heading('Background', level=1)

p('Comparing two cell populations is among the most common tasks in single-cell analysis. Researchers typically choose a standard metric: Euclidean distance, cosine similarity, Pearson or Spearman correlation, or Jensen-Shannon divergence. These metrics are convenient, but they treat all gene expression differences equally.')

p('Not all expression changes have the same biological meaning: a twofold change in GAPDH may reflect technical noise, whereas a twofold change in a transcription factor may reflect a functional state shift, and standard metrics cannot tell these apart. The problem is acute in large single-cell atlases [1], where donor- and batch-level variation often dominates over cell-type identity. Methods such as Harmony [2], scVI [3], and SATURN [4] remove such nuisance variation [5], but a key question remains: how much of the difference between two populations reflects functional adaptation rather than neutral drift?')

p('This question mirrors one addressed in molecular evolution. The Ka/Ks ratio (dN/dS) distinguishes nonsynonymous changes (Ka, which alter the protein) from synonymous changes (Ks, largely silent) [6,7], using synonymous sites as an internal baseline so that the ratio reveals selection. While CKI does not share Ka/Ks\u2019s formal mathematical properties (notably the shared mutation rate that cancels in the ratio; see Discussion), it adopts an analogous heuristic logic for transcriptomic comparisons.')

p('CKI defines two rates: a baseline divergence rate k_n, estimated from housekeeping (HK) gene expression, and a functional divergence rate k_f, estimated from cell-type identity genes. The ratio \u03c9 = k_f/k_n quantifies baseline-normalized functional divergence: values close to the equivalent-population calibration baseline (established empirically within each dataset; see Results) indicate baseline-consistent differences, values far above indicate functional divergence exceeding baseline variation, and values far below indicate strong functional constraint. The scope of \u03c9 is bounded by a single central assumption, stated here once and quantified empirically throughout: the HK anchor is valid only where housekeeping expression remains constrained. Functional signal located on HK genes is invisible to \u03c9 by construction, and in disease or strong-perturbation contexts the anchor itself shifts\u2014housekeeping dysregulation and composition change in cancer (TCGA, below), IFN-\u03b2 stimulation of housekeeping genes in the perturbation demonstration (below; simulated adversarial scenarios in Additional file 1: Note 3.12)\u2014inflating the denominator and deflating the ratio. In such regimes \u03c9 must be read alongside its components, and k_f with a design-matched null is the honest default for ordering claims (Results; Limitations). CKI \u03c9 is a heuristic index, not a formal measure of Darwinian selection; interpretation is anchored in the empirical distribution of \u03c9 within each analysis rather than in fixed ratio cut-offs or claims about selection regimes (see Discussion).')

p('We evaluated CKI across four scales. First, we calibrated CKI on Tabula Muris mouse data [8]: random splits of the same cell population yield \u03c9 above 1 (empirical calibration baseline 6.67, 95% bootstrap CI [4.24, 9.24]). Second, in Tabula Sapiens human data [9], CKI \u03c9 is negatively correlated with all four standard distance metrics; a decomposition analysis showed this association is partly driven by the k_n denominator (k_n is itself strongly positively correlated with the standard metrics), so \u03c9 is a composite of numerator and denominator information rather than a fully independent dimension (see Results). Third, applied to TCGA cancer data [10,11], CKI suggested that tumors appear more transcriptionally homogeneous than adjacent non-tumor tissues at bulk resolution (an exploratory observation; see Results for caveats). Fourth, in a human brain single-nucleus atlas [12], we measured how non-neuronal cell classes differ across brain regions, quantifying a 6.10-fold regional differentiation gradient and screening for anomalously similar cell-type/region-pair candidates; because no candidate survived false-discovery-rate correction under a strict block-shuffle null preserving the cell-type-by-region design, we present the brain candidates as hypothesis-generating signals for future lineage-tracing validation.')

# ============================================================
# RESULTS (NAR: placed AFTER Methods)
# ============================================================
heading('Results', level=1)

# --- Result 1 ---
heading('Decomposing transcriptomic variation', level=2)

p('CKI takes two cell populations as input, each represented as a pseudobulk expression vector (the mean expression across all cells in that population). The computation has three steps, all of which use the same metric (Jensen-Shannon divergence) on the same underlying expression matrix, ensuring the ratio is internally calibrated (Fig. 1).')

p('Step 1: Compute the baseline divergence rate k_n. We restrict the pseudobulk vectors to housekeeping (HK) gene indices and add a +1 pseudo-count followed by L1 normalization, which converts expression values to probabilities. k_n is the JS divergence between these two HK gene probability distributions. Because HK genes should not differ systematically between biologically equivalent populations [13], k_n captures baseline noise: technical variation, stochastic transcriptional bursting, and individual-level physiological differences.')

p('Step 2: Compute the functional divergence rate k_f. We restrict the pseudobulk vectors to identity gene indices\u2014genes that define cell-type-specific functions. In the default configuration (used for the Tabula Muris full pairwise matrix, Additional file 1: Fig. S1), identity genes are the top-2,000 highly variable genes (HVGs; Seurat flavor), excluding HK genes to maintain k_n/k_f independence. For the mouse pilot calibration and all other analyses (human Tabula Sapiens, TCGA, and brain atlas), we used a hybrid scheme: k_n is computed per pair on the shared HK gene set, while k_f uses the top-200 differentially expressed genes (ranked by absolute mean difference) for that specific pair, with HK genes excluded. k_f is the JS divergence between these two identity gene probability distributions.')

p(f'Step 3: \u03c9 = k_f/k_n. For statistical inference, we perform permutation testing (B = 1,000 for all datasets): group labels are permuted (cell labels for mouse and human, sample labels for TCGA) or, for the brain, library-to-region assignments are block-shuffled, and \u03c9 is recalculated to generate a null distribution. The empirical P-value is P = (count(\u03c9_null \u2265 \u03c9_obs) + 1)/(B + 1), and effect size is reported as standardized effect size (SES = (\u03c9_obs \u2212 \u03bc_null) / \u03c3_null) as a non-parametric descriptive statistic complementary to the permutation P-value. Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction is applied within each dataset. For the larger-scale analyses (Tabula Sapiens, TCGA, brain atlas), non-parametric statistical tests and descriptive statistics (median, IQR, effect sizes) are also reported.')

p(f'We ran a parameter sweep on Tabula Muris mouse data ({_sb["n_pairs"]} cell-type pairs across {_ds["tabula_muris_organs"]} organs) to test whether adding pathway enrichment scores to k_f would improve performance. We found that the identity-only configuration (w_identity = 1.0, w_pathway = 0.0) achieved the best cell-type discrimination (area under the ROC curve, AUC = {_sb["identity_auc"]:.3f}, Additional file 1: Fig. S1; Additional file 1: Table S1). CKI does not require external pathway databases to produce biologically meaningful results\u2014partitioning the expression data into constrained baseline and identity gene sets is sufficient.')

# --- Result 2 ---
heading('Calibration confirms baseline behavior', level=2)

p(f'We calibrated CKI on the Tabula Muris FACS dataset [8] (SmartSeq2, {_ds["tabula_muris_cells"]:,} cells, {_ds["tabula_muris_genes"]:,} genes, {_ds["tabula_muris_organs"]} organs). Housekeeping genes were loaded from the HRT Atlas v1.0 reference [13] (mouse ortholog column). For the full pairwise matrix (703 pairs, Additional file 1: Fig. S1), identity genes were the top-{_ds["n_hvg"]:,} highly variable genes (HVGs; Seurat), excluding HK genes. For the pilot calibration (controls, S/D/X categories), we used a hybrid scheme: per-pair k_n on the shared HK gene set with per-pair k_f (top-200 differentially expressed genes, ranked by absolute mean difference, HK excluded) (Fig. 2).')

p(f'We performed six control comparisons in which we randomly split the same cell population into two halves (Fig. 2A). The mean \u03c9 was {_mc["control_mean"]:.2f} (median {_mc["control_median"]:.2f}, range {_mc["control_min"]:.2f}\u2013{_mc["control_max"]:.2f}, CV \u2248 52%), indicating substantial variability in the empirical baseline estimate. None of the six comparisons reached statistical significance (all P > 0.05, one-sided permutation test). This indicates that CKI recognizes biologically equivalent cell populations as having no significant functional divergence. We note that while these results are consistent with baseline behavior, formal equivalence testing (e.g., two one-sided tests, TOST) with a larger calibration sample would provide stronger statistical evidence for equivalence; the current sample size (n = 6) with a coefficient of variation of ~52% limits the power of such tests and the precision of the calibration factor (\u03c9_cal = \u03c9 / 6.67).')

p(f'Beyond controls, \u03c9 values increased monotonically with biological distance (Fig. 2B, D). Same cell type across different organs (S category: mean \u03c9 = {_mc["S_mean"]:.2f}, n = {_mc["S_n"]} pairs) had lower \u03c9 than different cell types within the same organ (D category: mean \u03c9 = {_mc["D_mean"]:.2f}, n = {_mc["D_n"]} pairs). Because the pilot contains only 2\u20134 pairs per category, these category means carry wide bootstrap 95% CIs (S: {_mc["S_ci_lower"]:.1f}\u2013{_mc["S_ci_upper"]:.1f}; D: {_mc["D_ci_lower"]:.1f}\u2013{_mc["D_ci_upper"]:.1f}) and should be read as calibration-scale estimates rather than precise effect sizes. The component-level analysis confirmed that k_f was the driver: from controls to inter-cell-type comparisons, k_f increased roughly 400-fold (mean {_mc["kf_mean_ctrl"]:.4f} \u2192 {_mc["kf_mean_D"]:.2f}\u2013{_mc["kf_mean_X"]:.2f}), while k_n increased only {_mc["kn_fold_D"]:.0f}\u2013{_mc["kn_fold_X"]:.0f}-fold ({_mc["kn_mean_ctrl"]:.5f} \u2192 {_mc["kn_mean_D"]:.3f}\u2013{_mc["kn_mean_X"]:.3f}; control-to-D and control-to-X, respectively). This establishes that CKI measures functional divergence, not just total difference.')

p(f'Because the empirical baseline (\u03c9 = 6.67, 95% bootstrap CI [4.24, 9.24]) deviates substantially from the theoretical ideal (\u03c9 = 1), we introduce a calibrated omega, \u03c9_cal = \u03c9_obs / 6.67, reported to one significant figure as an order-of-magnitude estimate. Under this mouse-derived calibration, mouse controls yield \u03c9_cal = 1.0, the brain global mean becomes \u2248 6 (raw 38.55; range 4.2\u20139.1 across the baseline CI), astrocytes\u2014the most regionally divergent class\u2014\u2248 12 (raw 82.75; range 9.0\u201319.5), and the most constrained classes, Bergmann glia and vascular cells, \u2248 2 (raw 13.56). However, scheme-matched split-half calibration inside the brain atlas (29 populations with at least 200 nuclei; B = 50 splits per population) gave an internal baseline of 9.73 (95% bootstrap CI [9.03, 10.53]), approximately 1.5-fold higher than the mouse-derived factor, so the mouse-derived calibration overstates \u03c9_cal in the brain dataset. Under the brain-internal baseline the global mean corresponds to \u03c9_cal \u2248 4.0, astrocytes \u2248 8.5, and Bergmann glia \u2248 1.4: Bergmann glia\u2019s own region-clustered CI ([8.49, 19.52]) straddles the baseline, so the most constrained classes sit at, not above, the split-half expectation. The statement that all ten classes diverge beyond the brain-internal baseline holds only under the mouse-derived calibration (Additional file 1: Note 3.5). In Tabula Sapiens the internal baseline was 7.67 (95% bootstrap CI [7.39, 8.00]; 71 populations from the largest donor per group), inside the mouse-derived CI [4.24, 9.24]; the mouse-derived factor is therefore transferable to Tabula Sapiens but not to the brain atlas, and \u03c9_cal should be treated as a dataset-relative quantity rather than a universal constant. Class-specific split-half baselines derived from the same 29 split-half populations address the residual between-class heterogeneity (class-mean k_n spans 1.4\u20139.2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b3 across the ten classes): they range from 7.58 (microglia) to 12.52 (committed oligodendrocyte precursors)\u2014a 1.65-fold spread\u2014and the split-half k_f floor itself varies roughly 40-fold across classes, so the equivalent-population expectation is a class-specific quantity in both components; each class baseline rests on only 2\u20133 split-half populations (50 splits per population), so the calibrated levels carry non-trivial denominator uncertainty (per-class two-stage 95% CIs span, for example, [8.25, 10.32] for astrocytes and [8.71, 9.47] for Bergmann glia). Under per-class baselines the astrocyte-to-Bergmann-glia gradient is 5.99 (95% CI [4.12, 9.18] under a joint region-clustered bootstrap that resamples region pairs and split-half populations together; the narrower [4.86, 7.42] previously obtained under i.i.d. pair resampling ignores within-region correlation and is anti-conservative)\u2014the point estimate is unchanged, because the two classes\u2019 own baselines nearly coincide (9.26 vs 9.08)\u2014while the calibrated levels compress (astrocytes \u03c9_cal = 8.9; Bergmann glia \u03c9_cal = 1.49, joint 95% CI [0.99, 2.12], which no longer excludes 1). Bergmann glia is therefore borderline against its own class baseline (region-clustered CI [8.49, 19.52] versus baseline 9.08; with only 7 contributing regions the lower edge of this interval is itself sensitive to bootstrap resampling), and we recommend per-class calibration whenever within-dataset class contrasts, rather than cross-dataset level comparisons, are the target (Additional file 1: Note 3.5). Calibrated values are reported alongside raw \u03c9 in all key results and Additional file 1: Fig. S2, and calibrate_omega() is available in the CKI package. The baseline reflects a roughly constant inflation of k_f by identity-gene selection\u2014a mechanism different from the shared-mutation-rate cancellation in Ka/Ks\u2014which is why \u03c9_cal rather than raw \u03c9 is the operational scale.')

# --- Result 3 ---
heading('Correlation structure between CKI and standard metrics', level=2)

p(f'We extended CKI to the Tabula Sapiens human atlas [9] ({_ds["tabula_sapiens_cells"]:,} cells; {_ds["tabula_sapiens_organs"]} h5ad files, {_ds["tabula_sapiens_ct_entries"]} cell-type entries, {_h["n_ct_analyzed"]} of which passed the pairwise-analysis filters and contributed C({_h["n_ct_analyzed"]}, 2) = {_h["n_pairs_total"]:,} pairs across liver, kidney, heart, bone marrow, spleen, and lung). We used the same hybrid scheme as the mouse pilot: k_n computed per pair on the shared HK gene set, k_f from the top-200 differentially expressed genes for that specific pair; because \u03c9 is a ratio of JS divergences computed from the same pseudobulk expression space, the normalization remains internally valid. HK genes were loaded from the HRT Atlas v1.0 reference [13] (human column) (Fig. 3).')

p(f'Human \u03c9 values ranged from {_h["omega_min"]:.2f} to {_h["omega_max"]:.2f} (mean {_h["omega_mean"]:.2f}, median {_h["omega_median"]:.2f}, n = {_h["n_pairs_total"]:,} pairs). We deliberately refrain from comparing these absolute magnitudes with the mouse pilot X-category mean ({_mc["X_mean"]:.2f}): that summary rests on only n = 2 pairs of a different pair composition on a different dataset scale, and cross-dataset \u03c9 comparisons are interpreted as rank-based only throughout (see Discussion). Within the human atlas, the biological hierarchy was preserved: same cell type across organs (mean \u03c9 = {_h["diff_organ_same_ct_mean"]:.2f}, n = {_h["diff_organ_same_ct_n"]} pairs) was lower than different cell types within the same organ (mean \u03c9 = {_h["same_organ_diff_ct_mean"]:.2f}, n = {_h["same_organ_diff_ct_n"]:,} pairs).')

_decomp_kf_lo = _decomp['corr_kf_M'].min(); _decomp_kf_hi = _decomp['corr_kf_M'].max()
_decomp_kn_lo = _decomp['corr_kn_M'].min(); _decomp_kn_hi = _decomp['corr_kn_M'].max()
_decomp_pc_lo = _decomp['partial_corr_omega_M_given_kn'].min()
_decomp_pc_hi = _decomp['partial_corr_omega_M_given_kn'].max()
p(f'We computed five metrics on all {_h["n_pairs_total"]:,} human cell-type pairs: CKI \u03c9, raw JS divergence (all genes), Spearman distance, cosine distance, and marker Jaccard distance. CKI \u03c9 was negatively correlated with all four standard metrics (Spearman r = {_sc["max"]:.2f} to {_sc["min"]:.2f}, all P < 0.001), whereas the four standard metrics formed a tight positive cluster (pairwise r = {_sc["std_pairwise_min"]:.2f}\u2013{_sc["std_pairwise_max"]:.2f}). Because \u03c9 = k_f/k_n is a ratio, a negative correlation can arise mechanically when the denominator k_n is itself positively correlated with those metrics (the classical spurious correlation of ratios). Decomposing the association: k_f was positively correlated with the standard metrics (Spearman r = +{_decomp_kf_lo:.2f} to +{_decomp_kf_hi:.2f}) and k_n more strongly so (r = +{_decomp_kn_lo:.2f} to +{_decomp_kn_hi:.2f}); the Spearman correlation between \u03c9 and each standard metric conditional on k_n became positive in all four cases (partial r = +{_decomp_pc_lo:.2f} to +{_decomp_pc_hi:.2f}, all P < 1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u2074; 95% bootstrap CIs excluding zero). The negative raw correlation is thus partly a ratio artifact of the k_n denominator, not evidence that \u03c9 measures a fully independent information dimension: \u03c9 combines k_f and k_n into a composite signal whose value lies in normalizing functional divergence against a population-specific baseline rather than in statistical independence from existing metrics.')

add_table_1(doc)
p(f'CKI showed moderate cell-type classification performance (AUC = {_au["cki_omega"]:.3f}, ranked 5th of 5 methods, below Spearman distance at AUC = {_au["spearman"]:.3f} and cosine distance at AUC = {_au["cosine"]:.3f}; Table 1; Additional file 1: Fig. S3). Pair-level bootstrap 95% CIs (B = 2,000): CKI \u03c9 [0.609, 0.745]; under a cell-type-entry clustered bootstrap the interval widens to [0.540, 0.804], its lower bound only marginally above the 0.5 chance level, so the classification advantage is weak and dependent on the resampling unit. The lower ranking is expected by design: CKI down-weights shared HK gene patterns to isolate functional divergence, trading global-transcriptional-identity sensitivity for identity-gene divergence. Critically, CKI was the only metric where same-organ different-cell-type pairs had higher values than different-organ different-cell-type pairs (mean \u03c9 {_h["same_organ_diff_ct_mean"]:.2f}, n = {_h["same_organ_diff_ct_n"]:,} vs. {_h["diff_organ_diff_ct_mean"]:.2f}, n = {_h["diff_organ_diff_ct_n"]:,}; all different-organ pairs combined: {_h["diff_organ_all_mean"]:.2f}, n = {_h["diff_organ_all_n"]:,}; Mann-Whitney U, P < 0.001). All four standard metrics showed the opposite pattern. A component decomposition tempers the functional reading of this reversal (companion pair set computed with a marginally less restrictive cell-count filter\u2014n = 1,140 same-organ and 4,011 different-organ pairs versus 1,038 and 3,813 in the main analysis; direction and magnitude unaffected): same-organ pairs have k_f indistinguishable from different-organ pairs (mean 0.247 vs. 0.250, Mann-Whitney P = 0.60) but significantly lower k_n (0.0130 vs. 0.0148, P = 3.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u2076). On the log scale the numerator contributes \u22120.005 (factor 0.99) while the smaller denominator contributes +0.176 (factor 1.19)\u2014essentially all of the reversal. The reversal therefore indicates a more stable housekeeping baseline within organs, not greater functional specialization of same-organ cell types; conditional on k_n, same-organ pairs retain only a small residual k_f elevation (partial regression coefficient +0.074, P = 9.8 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u2070).')

# --- Result 3b: ground-truth simulation ---
heading('Ground-truth simulation dissociates specificity from sensitivity', level=2)

p('No real dataset provides pairs with known functional divergence, so we built a semi-synthetic ground truth by injecting perturbations of known magnitude into a real single-cell background (Tabula Muris FACS marrow B cells; both groups of each comparison resampled from the same cell type, so the true pre-injection divergence is zero; Methods). Under pure neutral housekeeping-gene drift, raw JS divergence and cosine distance exceeded their null thresholds in 55% and 58% of replicates\u2014they cannot distinguish neutral drift from functional divergence\u2014whereas \u03c9 produced false positives in 0% of replicates (2% under global overdispersion noise). Exact Clopper-Pearson intervals confirm these rates: \u03c9 exceeded its threshold in 0 of 150 pure HK-drift replicates (one-sided 95% upper bound 0.020) and 2 of 250 pooled neutral replicates (95% CI [0.001, 0.029]), versus 0.553 [0.470, 0.635] for raw JS and 0.580 [0.497, 0.660] for cosine. Conversely, detecting an injected functional module required strong signals: at \u03b4 = 1 (a twofold shift on 200 genes), raw JS detected 99% and cosine 67% of replicates, whereas \u03c9 detected none (0 of 150) and k_f alone detected 2 of 150 (95% CI [0.002, 0.047]); only at \u03b4 = 2 did \u03c9 exceed threshold in 13% of replicates (20 of 150; 95% CI [0.083, 0.198]; range 12\u201316% across module seeds). This insensitivity is structural: the per-pair top-200 selection saturates with noise even under the null (median baseline k_f = 0.025 on this background), so weak module shifts do not lift k_f above the selection floor\u2014a simulation-level confirmation that k_f behaves as an upper-bound estimator. When signal and neutral drift co-occurred (\u03b4 = 0.5 with \u03b7 = 0.25\u20131), \u03c9 detection was further suppressed by the inflated denominator, whereas cosine and raw JS detection rose from 14% to 100%, because they count neutral drift as divergence. Because the neutral-drift null is itself defined on HK genes\u2014the same anchoring assumption CKI makes\u2014these specificity and discrimination results are conditional on that assumption; two adversarial scenarios quantify the boundary (Additional file 1: Note 3.12). When the functional module was instead placed on HK genes, \u03c9 detected none of the signals (rate 0.000 at every \u03b4 \u2265 0.25, while k_n itself fired at 0.61\u20131.00 and unnormalized metrics detected moderate-to-strong signals), so \u03c9 is structurally blind to functional change located on its anchor genes; conversely, when neutral drift was placed on non-HK genes, \u03c9\u2019s false-positive rate stayed at or below 0.02 (versus 1.00 for raw JS at \u03b7 = 1), so \u03c9 trades an HK-located blind spot for strong specificity against off-anchor drift\u2014conservative by construction.')

p('Integrating specificity and sensitivity, \u03c9 achieved the highest area under the ROC curve for discriminating functional (\u03b4 \u2265 0.25) from neutral perturbations (AUC = 0.80, versus 0.72 for k_f alone, 0.64 for raw JS, 0.58 for cosine, 0.44 for k_f/k_total, and 0.21 for k_n alone; replicate-block-stratified bootstrap 95% CIs: \u03c9 0.804 [0.777, 0.831], k_f 0.716 [0.681, 0.748], with the \u03c9 advantage over k_f holding in 100% of resamples): the apparent power of the standard metrics is purchased with false positives on neutral drift. The ratio construction also conferred robustness to technical asymmetry: under a fourfold cell-count imbalance (n_B = 50), \u03c9 changed by \u221232% (8.47 versus 12.46 for the signal-only \u03b4 = 1 comparison) while k_f inflated by +67% and cosine distance by +108%, and neither 30% dropout nor a twofold depth difference produced a systematic shift. The baseline \u03c9 of the simulation background (mean 11.6, 95th percentile 20.8) reproduced the scale of the brain-internal split-half calibration (9.73), supporting the external validity of the setup. These results delimit the intended use of \u03c9: a specificity-first screen whose construction rejects neutral drift, at the cost of bounded power for weak-to-moderate functional signals (Additional file 1: Note 3.12). That power bound is itself background-dependent: the second background (skin keratinocyte stem cells; Results below) has a lower k_f selection floor (median baseline k_f 0.011 versus 0.025 in marrow), and at \u03b4 = 1 \u03c9 detection power reached 0.91 there versus 0.00 in marrow.')

p('To exclude background specificity, the entire simulation was repeated in a second background within the same Tabula Muris FACS platform\u2014skin keratinocyte stem cells (1,371 cells; epithelial versus immune lineage, different tissue and sequencing depth)\u2014with the identical design (1,750 replicates; Methods). The \u03c9 advantage was fully reproduced: AUC(\u03c9) = 0.908 versus AUC(k_f) = 0.859, with the same strict metric ranking in both backgrounds (marrow: \u03c9 0.804 > k_f 0.716 > raw JS 0.640 > cosine 0.584 > k_f/k_total 0.437 > k_n 0.213; skin: \u03c9 0.908 > k_f 0.859 > raw JS 0.649 > cosine 0.608 > k_f/k_total 0.539 > k_n 0.207) and \u03c9 AUC gains over k_f of +0.088 (marrow) and +0.049 (skin). The known limitations replicated as well: under strong confounding (\u03b4 = 0.5 with \u03b7 \u2265 0.25 neutral housekeeping drift) both \u03c9 and k_f fell below the detection threshold, and under the fourfold cell-count imbalance (n_B = 50) k_f retained higher power than \u03c9 (detection rate 0.98\u20131.00 versus 0.04\u20130.20 in the skin background), because the ratio construction adds the variance of the k_n denominator to the estimator\u2014the flip side of its immunity to neutral drift. The advantage of \u03c9 thus lies in discriminating functional signal from neutral drift, not in raw detection power for uncontaminated signals.')

# --- Result 3b: real perturbation demonstration (IFN-beta PBMC) ---
heading('Real perturbation demonstration: IFN-\u03b2-stimulated PBMCs', level=2)
p('The simulation leaves open how \u03c9 behaves on a real perturbation whose ground truth is known but whose effect on the housekeeping anchor is not. We re-analyzed peripheral blood mononuclear cells from eight donors, control versus 6-hour IFN-\u03b2 stimulation [14] (24,413 singlet cells across six cell types; pseudobulk per donor and condition; Methods). Because the control and stimulated conditions were captured in separate 10x lanes, condition is fully confounded with lane, and every metric inherits the same confound; we therefore read the analysis as a relative architecture demonstration (metric versus metric within one confounded design), not as an absolute detection claim. Under that reading, the perturbation was visible at the \u03c9 level in all six cell types: the median \u03c9 between stimulated and control pseudobulks of the same donor exceeded the median donor-versus-donor \u03c9 (within condition, across donors) by 1.1\u20132.0-fold and lay 1.8\u20133.4-fold above the split-half baseline; the within-donor permutation test reached raw P < 0.05 in 15 of 37 donor-level tests (several at the permutation floor; no multiplicity correction\u2014under the global null, roughly two of 37 tests would cross raw P < 0.05, so the aggregate excess is informative while individual tests are not). However, k_f alone separated the perturbation class from the donor-drift class as well or better (exact rank AUC across cell types: \u03c9 0.55\u20130.92, k_f 0.74\u20131.00, raw JS 0.79\u20131.00), and in CD14+ monocytes the \u03c9 AUC fell to 0.55 while k_f retained 0.98. The components are consistent with the anchor-visibility mechanism\u2014IFN-\u03b2 stimulation raises housekeeping expression itself (median k_n rises 1.2\u20135.7-fold above the donor-drift level; ACTB and GAPDH fall by roughly 40% in stimulated cells, a magnitude consistent with the mechanism but not separable from the lane effect)\u2014so the denominator inflates and partially cancels the functional signal, most strongly in monocytes. The demonstration thus supports, within a design whose absolute levels are lane-confounded, the anchor-visibility boundary stated in the decision rules (when the perturbation touches the housekeeping anchor, the ratio deflates and k_f-only with a design-matched null is the more honest statistic), while providing evidence consistent with \u03c9 tracking real perturbation signal above donor drift at calibrated levels when the anchor is unaffected. Scripts and outputs: notebooks/79_kang_ifnb_demo.py; results/kang_ifnb_demo_pairs.csv; results/kang_ifnb_demo_summary.json (Additional file 1: Fig. S13; Additional file 1: Note 3.15).')

# --- Result 3c: fixed gene-panel ablation ---
heading('Fixed gene-panel ablation: rankings robust, absolute \u03c9 scheme-specific', level=2)
p('The reference implementation first reproduced the reported landscape exactly (maximum per-pair |\u0394\u03c9| = 6.4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u00b3 over all 31,764 pairs). Replacing the circular per-pair panel with non-circular alternatives left rankings essentially untouched: pair-level \u03c9 under the leave-pair-out scheme correlated with the reported scheme at Spearman \u03c1 = 0.937 (\u03c1 = 0.918 for the fixed 2,000-gene panel, 0.931 for all 5,000 non-HK genes), and the ordering of the ten class means at \u03c1 = 0.99 under leave-pair-out (0.90 and 0.93 under the fixed and unselected panels). The astrocyte-to-Bergmann-glia gradient\u2014the headline brain result\u2014was preserved and, under leave-pair-out, amplified (6.10-fold reported; 6.53-fold leave-pair-out, 7.67-fold fixed panel, 6.57-fold unselected), because non-circular panels deflate \u03c9 more strongly in transcriptionally constrained classes. Class-level significance was largely robust under a scheme-matched block-shuffle null (B = 200) with leave-pair-out panels: astrocytes, OPCs, and committed OPCs remained at the permutation floor (P \u2264 1/(B + 1) = 0.005), fibroblasts remained significant (P = 0.020, versus 0.030 reported), vascular cells\u2014non-significant under the reported scheme (P = 0.115)\u2014reached significance (P = 0.035), ependymal cells were not significant (P = 0.164), and the remaining classes (microglia P = 0.796, oligodendrocytes P = 0.876, choroid plexus P = 0.562, Bergmann glia P = 0.980) stayed clearly non-significant.')

p('The ablation also quantifies what circular selection costs. On the same pairs, the circular panel inflated k_f by a median of 1.61-fold relative to the leave-pair-out panel (IQR 1.27\u20132.07) and by 6.1- and 7.3-fold relative to the fixed and unselected panels (which also differ in panel composition), confirming that reported k_f magnitudes\u2014and therefore absolute \u03c9\u2014are upper-bound estimates whose scale is scheme-specific (grand mean 38.55 reported; 26.5 leave-pair-out; 6.5 fixed; 5.3 unselected). Two consequences follow: rank-based conclusions are robust (the multiplicative-residual ranking\u2014the basis of the region-association screen\u2014correlated at \u03c1 = 0.86\u20130.88 across schemes, and the leave-pair-out scheme retained 32 of 50 (64%) of the reported residual < 0.3 candidate pairs, although the same Strong rule is passed by 457 pairs on the leave-pair-out scale), but absolute thresholds do not transfer: the tier cutoffs (\u03c9 < 15/25/35) are calibrated to the reported scheme\u2019s inflated scale, so tier counts are not comparable across gene-selection schemes and cross-scheme comparisons must be made on ranks (Additional file 1: Note 3.13).')

# --- Result 4 ---
heading('Cancer analysis suggests apparent tumor homogeneity (exploratory)', level=2)

p(f'We applied CKI to TCGA bulk RNA-seq data across five cancer types (LUAD, LUSC, LIHC, KIRC, BRCA) [10,11], totalling {_tc["n_total"]:,} samples. We asked a simple question: when cancer develops, how much functional transcriptional divergence occurs? CKI provides a principled answer by comparing tumor-tumor (TT), normal-normal (NN), and tumor-normal (TN) \u03c9 values (Fig. 4; Additional file 1: Fig. S4).')

p('The TCGA analysis is exploratory: bulk RNA-seq cannot distinguish genuine transcriptional convergence of tumor cells from cell-composition shifts (tumor purity, stromal and immune infiltration), peritumoral inflammation shared across tumors, or systematic RNA quality differences between tumor and normal specimens; moreover, TCGA "normal" samples are adjacent non-tumor tissues from cancer patients, not healthy donor tissue, so NN comparisons quantify heterogeneity among adjacent non-tumor specimens. With these caveats, a notable observation, at bulk RNA-seq resolution, was that tumors appeared more transcriptionally homogeneous than adjacent non-tumor tissues: in all five cancer types the median NN/TT \u03c9 ratio exceeded 1.0. Liver cancer (LIHC) showed the smallest contrast (median NN/TT = 1.23), lung adenocarcinoma (LUAD) the largest (median NN/TT = 2.32), with intermediate values for BRCA (1.51), LUSC (1.77), and KIRC (2.19). Component decomposition shows this ratio is predominantly a denominator effect: tumor-tumor pairs have higher k_f (NN/TT k_f ratio 0.54\u20130.74) and normal-normal pairs lower k_n (NN/TT k_n ratio 0.27\u20130.46), so the \u03c9 reversal reflects a more stable housekeeping baseline among adjacent non-tumor specimens rather than smaller functional-gene divergence of tumor cells. A four-panel marker composition check (immune, myeloid, stromal, epithelial) with sample-level cluster bootstrap intervals finds the pooled tumor-pair k_n coefficient essentially unchanged after composition adjustment (\u22120.5%, 95% CI [\u22123.2%, +2.6%]; per cancer type \u221214% to +34%), so marker-measurable composition explains little of the pooled reversal, although as a noisy proxy it bounds rather than settles the question (Additional file 1: Note 5.2). Single-cell or deconvolution-based analyses would be needed to disentangle these alternatives.')

p('Paired tumor-normal comparisons yielded higher \u03c9 than unpaired comparisons in four of five cancer types (paired/unpaired ratio = 0.89\u20131.54, where paired comparisons are same-patient tumor-normal pairs and the unpaired comparator averages all cross-patient tumor-normal pairs, not (mean \u03c9_TT + mean \u03c9_NN)/2). However, with only n = 2\u20135 paired samples per cancer type, formal hypothesis testing lacks adequate statistical power (minimum two-sided P \u2248 0.33 for n = 2); we therefore report these comparisons as descriptive statistics only, without P-values. Definitive conclusions about within-patient versus between-patient variation require larger paired cohorts.')

p('Within cancer types, \u03c9 tracked clinical severity, although component controls show these gradients are predominantly baseline (k_n) effects rather than functional-divergence orderings (below; Additional file 1: Note 3.16). In liver cancer, \u03c9 decreased with Edmondson grade [15]: G1 (73.5 \u00b1 15.6, n = 39) > G2 (67.0 \u00b1 17.7, n = 133) > G3 (66.2 \u00b1 17.1, n = 105) \u2248 G4 (66.2 \u00b1 24.5, n = 11; Jonckheere-Terpstra trend test, P < 0.0001). PAM50 subtype analysis [16,17] in breast cancer revealed a heterogeneity gradient: Luminal A highest (123.4 \u00b1 36.2, n = 224), then Luminal B (116.6 \u00b1 26.9, n = 123), HER2-enriched (103.1 \u00b1 29.4, n = 55), Basal-like (97.8 \u00b1 27.1, n = 97), and Normal-like lowest (90.5 \u00b1 44.6, n = 7; Kruskal-Wallis, P = 7.9 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u2070). LUAD mutation strata differed (Kruskal-Wallis, P = 2.1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076), with KRAS-mutant (118.1 \u00b1 27.7, n = 120) and EGFR-mutant tumors (107.0 \u00b1 24.8, n = 61) above wild-type (100.5 \u00b1 33.9, n = 311). The smallest subgroups (Normal-like n = 7; Edmondson G4 n = 11) have limited power (we attach no significance to their ordering relative to adjacent subtypes). Component controls (k_f-only and k_n, identical pipeline; Additional file 1: Note 3.16) qualify all three gradients. In liver cancer the direction reverses under k_f alone (mean k_f rises from 0.50 in G1 to 0.53 in G4; Jonckheere P = 1.1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u00b2, while k_n rises from 0.0087 to 0.0112), so the \u03c9 decrease is a denominator effect. In breast cancer the PAM50 ordering largely reverses under k_f-only (Luminal A lowest mean k_f, 0.479; Basal-like highest, 0.536; Kruskal-Wallis P = 1.3 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u00b9), consistent with the lowest k_n in Luminal A (0.0049) and the highest in Basal-like (0.0061), so the \u03c9 heterogeneity gradient is predominantly a baseline effect. Only the LUAD mutation contrast persists under k_f-only (Kruskal-Wallis P = 0.015), with KRAS-mutant tumors highest in both \u03c9 and k_f, while the EGFR elevation above wild-type is not (EGFR-mutant tumors carry the lowest mean k_f, 0.472). We therefore read the severity gradients as exploratory and predominantly (rather than exclusively) denominator-dominated: they describe how the ratio behaves across strata rather than a functional-divergence ordering\u2014the LUAD mutation contrast, which persists under k_f-only with KRAS-mutant tumors highest in both metrics, is the one exception\u2014and the PAM50 gradient may additionally reflect proliferative-fraction differences (Basal-like tumors proliferate fastest); the analysis remains exploratory for the reasons given above.')

# --- Result 5 ---
heading('CKI ranks cell types by cross-organ conservation', level=2)

add_table_2(doc)
p(f'Among the {_h["n_pairs_total"]:,} Tabula Sapiens cell-type pairs, {DATA["cross_organ_n_total"]} are same-cell-type cross-organ comparisons. These pairs allowed us to ask: which cell types maintain their transcriptional identity regardless of where they reside, and which are strongly shaped by their organ environment (Fig. 5; Table 2; Additional file 1: Fig. S5)?')

p(f'The cross-organ \u03c9 ranking reveals a broad spectrum of conservation across {len(t2)} cell types under the composite \u03c9 ordering, of which only the extremes reproduce under k_f (k_f-only controls below; Additional file 1: Note 3.16) (Table 2). Because single-pair means are statistically uninformative, we anchor interpretation on well-sampled cell types (n \u2265 5 pairs; upper block of Table 2): {t2[0][0]}s (mean \u03c9 = {t2[0][1]} \u00b1 {t2[0][2]}, n = {t2[0][3]}) and {t2[1][0]}s (mean \u03c9 = {t2[1][1]} \u00b1 {t2[1][2]}, n = {t2[1][3]}) were the most conserved, followed by {t2[2][0]}s (mean {t2[2][1]} \u00b1 {t2[2][2]}, n = {t2[2][3]}) and {t2_next[0]}s (mean {t2_next[1]} \u00b1 {t2_next[2]}, n = {t2_next[3]}); {mac[0]}s, the most abundant type in cross-organ comparisons (n = {mac[3]}), showed intermediate conservation (mean {mac[1]} \u00b1 {mac[2]}). At the divergent end, {last2[0][0]}s (mean {last2[0][1]} \u00b1 {last2[0][2]}, n = {last2[0][3]}) and {last2[1][0]}s (mean {last2[1][1]} \u00b1 {last2[1][2]}, n = {last2[1][3]}) had the highest cross-organ \u03c9, consistent with the organ-specific gene programs of endothelial cells [18], although both are represented by only n = 3 pairs and this end of the ranking is suggestive only. Among sparsely sampled cell types (n = 1\u20133 pairs, lower block of Table 2), {t2_sparse[0][0]}s and {t2_sparse[1][0]}s show the lowest single-pair \u03c9 values, but with one pair each these estimates carry no reliable biological signal.')

p(f'The cross-organ conservation ranking from CKI showed little agreement with rankings from standard metrics (Spearman r = {min(DATA["cross_organ_spearman"].values()):.2f} to {max(DATA["cross_organ_spearman"].values()):.2f}, n = {DATA["cross_organ_n_total"]} pairs; bootstrap 95% CIs reported in Additional file 1: Table S2). This is because CKI explicitly normalizes: two cell populations might share similar highly expressed genes (yielding high Jaccard similarity), but if their constrained baseline k_n is low, even modest functional differences can produce a high \u03c9. This normalization reveals patterns that raw expression similarity misses. A k_f-only control qualifies the ranking\u2019s interpretation (Additional file 1: Note 3.16): per-cell-type mean \u03c9 and mean k_f are only weakly concordant (Spearman r = 0.23, P = 0.37, n = 17 cell types; r = 0.10 among the five well-sampled types; per-pair r = 0.44, P = 5.7 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074, n = 59). CD8+ T cells are the most conserved well-sampled type under both \u03c9 and k_f, and the divergent end is directionally consistent (endothelial cells and erythrocytes carry the highest mean k_f among multi-pair types), but the middle of the ordering does not reproduce (NK cells rank most divergent under \u03c9 yet second-most conserved under k_f among well-sampled types), so the cross-organ ranking should be read as a composite of functional divergence and baseline differences.')

# --- Result 6 ---
heading('Brain regional analysis reveals cell-type divergence gradients', level=2)

p('We applied CKI to the Siletti et al. human brain single-nucleus RNA-seq atlas [12], which profiles ~3.3 million nuclei across 108 brain regions, asking how much functional divergence separates the same cell type across regions. We focused on the 888,263 non-neuronal nuclei spanning 10 major cell classes (astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, oligodendrocyte precursors, microglia, vascular cells, fibroblasts, ependymal cells, choroid plexus, committed oligodendrocyte precursors, and Bergmann glia); 886,808 nuclei in groups passing the >= 20-nuclei filter contributed to the analysis. Neurons were excluded because the supercluster_term annotation does not resolve neuronal subtype heterogeneity (glutamatergic, GABAergic, dopaminergic, spanning dozens of transcriptionally distinct populations), and treating neurons as a single class would violate the same-cell-type assumption, as different regions contain fundamentally different neuron subtype compositions; the non-neuronal classes provide a practical aggregation level for cross-region comparison. CKI \u03c9 was computed for all same-cell-type cross-region comparisons (31,764 pairs total) (Fig. 6; Additional file 1: Fig. S6; Additional file 1: Table S3). These classes are not equally well defined: vascular cells and fibroblasts are heterogeneous superclusters that likely aggregate multiple transcriptionally distinct subtypes (perivascular and meningeal populations among others), so their class-level \u03c9 values represent supercluster averages rather than properties of a single coherent cell type.')

p('Mean \u03c9 spanned a 6.10-fold gradient across cell classes. Bergmann glia and vascular cells showed the lowest mean \u03c9 (both 13.56; Bergmann glia 13.56 \u00b1 5.58, n = 21 pairs across 7 regions\u2014all intra-cerebellar, so their low \u03c9 partly reflects this anatomical restriction; vascular cells 13.56 \u00b1 4.78, n = 3,321 pairs across 82 regions). They were followed by fibroblasts (18.18 \u00b1 9.59, n = 3,403 pairs across 83 regions), ependymal cells (22.98 \u00b1 10.14, n = 780 pairs across 40 regions), microglia (24.31 \u00b1 12.35, n = 5,671 pairs across 107 regions), and committed oligodendrocyte precursors (28.84 \u00b1 12.51, n = 1,326 pairs across 52 regions); oligodendrocytes (37.05 \u00b1 15.73, n = 5,778 pairs across 108 regions), choroid plexus (37.76 \u00b1 15.41, n = 15 pairs across 6 regions; too sparsely sampled to rank reliably), and oligodendrocyte precursors (40.62 \u00b1 19.89, n = 5,671 pairs across 107 regions) showed intermediate-to-high divergence. Astrocytes were the most regionally divergent (82.75 \u00b1 44.98, n = 5,778 pairs across 108 regions), a 6.10-fold increase over Bergmann glia and vascular cells (under class-specific split-half baselines the gradient is 5.99, 95% CI [4.12, 9.18] under the joint region-clustered bootstrap; see Discussion). Region-clustered block bootstrap 95% CIs for the class-level means (B = 2,000; Methods): astrocytes 82.75 [75.66, 89.53], oligodendrocyte precursors 40.62 [37.02, 44.21], oligodendrocytes 37.05 [34.31, 39.88], microglia 24.31 [21.80, 26.87], Bergmann glia 13.56 [8.49, 19.52]. Excluding Bergmann glia the gradient remains 6.10-fold (astrocyte versus vascular cells; pair-level bootstrap 95% CI [6.00, 6.21]); the region-clustered CI for the full 10-class gradient is 6.10 [5.55, 9.63].')

p('To test whether this gradient exceeds chance, we used a cell-type-level block-shuffle null that permutes 10x-library-to-region assignments (B = 1,000; Methods). For 4 of 10 cell classes, regional structure significantly raised mean \u03c9 (astrocytes: one-sided P \u2264 1/(B + 1) = 9.99 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074, the permutation floor, SES = 6.49; oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs): P = 0.002, SES = 4.04; committed OPCs: P = 0.005, SES = 2.60; fibroblasts: P = 0.030, SES = 1.83). Ependymal cells were borderline (P = 0.075, SES = 1.52) and vascular cells non-significant (P = 0.115, SES = 1.24), both reflecting low effect sizes. For three classes the observed mean fell below the null expectation (microglia P = 0.904, SES = \u22121.25; oligodendrocytes P = 0.883, SES = \u22121.15; Bergmann glia P = 0.998, SES = \u22122.76), and choroid plexus was not significant (P = 0.566, n = 15 pairs). Under Benjamini-Hochberg correction across the m = 10 non-neuronal classes, astrocytes and OPCs have q = 0.010 (a censored upper bound, computed from the floor P), committed OPCs q = 0.017, and fibroblasts q = 0.075. A donor-stratified null sensitivity analysis (Methods) gives 5 of 10 classes with raw P < 0.05\u2014including ependymal cells (P = 0.021) and fibroblasts (P = 0.048)\u2014of which the same 3 of 10 survive Benjamini-Hochberg correction (ependymal cells q = 0.052 and fibroblasts q = 0.096 do not pass). The gradient is thus supported at the cell-class level for a subset of classes\u2014most strongly astrocytes and the OPC-to-oligodendrocyte lineage\u2014while microglia, oligodendrocytes, and Bergmann glia sit at or below the null expectation.')

p('Two analyses probe the robustness of the gradient. First, because the atlas contains nuclei from only four donors and 94.5% of region pairs share at least one donor, the pooled gradient could reflect donor identity rather than regional biology. Restricting comparisons to same-donor region pairs (within-donor cross-region analysis; Methods) preserved the extremes: astrocytes remained the most divergent class (mean \u03c9 = 75.18 across 11,139 within-donor pairs, versus 82.75 pooled) and Bergmann glia the least (16.73), a 4.50-fold gradient. The middle of the ordering is noisier (oligodendrocytes 56.93 versus 37.05; vascular cells 20.89 versus 13.56; OPCs 26.34 versus 40.62). Donor sharing cannot explain the gradient away, although within-donor values are systematically lower for the largest classes, indicating some between-donor contribution. Second, component decomposition shows the astrocyte-versus-Bergmann-glia contrast is predominantly a k_n effect: k_f differs only 2.0-fold (0.135 vs. 0.067) while mean k_n differs 3.2-fold (1.81 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b3 vs. 5.83 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b3); multiplicatively, the component product is 2.03 \u00d7 3.21 = 6.51, close to but not identical with the 6.10 ratio of class means (the gap arises because the mean of per-pair ratios is not the ratio of component means; Discussion). Across the ten classes, class-mean \u03c9 tracks k_n (Spearman \u03c1 = \u22120.73, P = 0.02) but not k_f (\u03c1 = 0.09, P = 0.80), and under a k_f-only ordering astrocytes rank third of ten (k_f-only gradient 4.1-fold; k_n-only 6.7-fold). The gradient chiefly reflects how stably housekeeping programs are maintained across regions, not proportionally larger functional-gene divergence. The ordering is stable under an aggregate-first k_n estimator (Spearman \u03c1 = 0.988 versus the per-pair estimator, gradient 6.51-fold) but not under a single global k_n shared by all classes (\u03c1 = 0.09), because that variant rescales k_f alone. Across all 31,764 brain comparisons, per-pair k_n also showed substantial cross-pair variability (CV = 97.52%), and the Spearman correlation between per-pair \u03c9 and global-k_n \u03c9 was only 0.142 (P = 7.07 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u2074\u00b3; Additional file 1: Fig. S7), so fine-grained \u03c9 orderings depend materially on the per-pair k_n estimator. We therefore recommend interpreting class-level \u03c9 as a composite signal and comparing k_f and k_n directly when mechanistic attribution matters.')

p('The gradient aligns with known cell biology. Vascular cells and fibroblasts are tissue-resident populations maintained and replenished locally; their low \u03c9 indicates broadly conserved core transcriptional programs across brain regions\u2014though low class-level \u03c9 is not spatial uniformity, since the blood-brain barrier is itself molecularly heterogeneous between regions [19] and meningeal structure differs across compartments [20]; such regional specialization is small relative to the transcriptional differences that define distinct cell identities. Microglia, the brain\u2019s resident immune cells, showed intermediate divergence: their phenotypes vary regionally but their core surveillance and phagocytic machinery is shared [21]. Oligodendrocyte myelination can be tuned to regional circuit demands, and astrocytes express region-specific sets of ion channels, neurotransmitter transporters, and secreted factors tailored to local circuit demands\u2014regional specialization compatible with the composite \u03c9 ordering but not established by it, because the ordering is predominantly k_n-driven (\u03c1(\u03c9, k_f class means) = 0.09; astrocytes rank third of ten under k_f alone), so the gradient should not be read as a ranking of functional divergence. The grand mean \u03c9 across all 31,764 pairs (38.55) is lower than the astrocyte mean (82.75) because it is dominated by cell types with many pairs and lower \u03c9 (e.g., vascular cells n = 3,321 at 13.56, microglia n = 5,671 at 24.31), a consequence of the skewed distribution of pair counts across cell types.')

p('The cross-region \u03c9 gradient provides a framework for generating hypotheses about cell residence and migration history: cell types that recently migrated to new regions or continuously exchange between regions should show low inter-regional \u03c9, while types stably resident in specific regions should accumulate regional transcriptional signatures. The framework must be applied with cell biology in mind. Vascular cells and fibroblasts are tissue-resident and replenished locally, so their low \u03c9 is more parsimoniously explained by local maintenance of a conserved structural program than by inter-regional exchange. Conversely, OPCs\u2014the most actively migrating non-neuronal population\u2014show the second-highest global mean \u03c9 (40.62), and the corrected candidate screen shows no oligodendrocyte-lineage concentration (fold enrichment 0.77, P = 0.92): these data provide no evidence that migration history shapes \u03c9 at either the pair or the class level. Because no individual candidate signal survives multiple-testing correction (below), we present this framework as hypothesis generation complementing lineage tracing and developmental studies rather than as inference.')

heading('Anomalously similar cell-type/region pairs: a hypothesis-generating screen', level=2)

p('Low CKI \u03c9 between a cell type across two brain regions indicates transcriptomic similarity beyond baseline expectation. Such similarity can arise from at least four non-exclusive mechanisms: (i) developmental origin heterogeneity\u2014cells from different embryonic progenitor pools (e.g., dorsal vs. ventral) retain distinct adult transcriptomic identities; (ii) embryonic colonization route boundaries\u2014immune cells that entered through different developmental entry points [22-24] show residual transcriptomic discontinuities; (iii) compartmentalized developmental specification\u2014region-specific developmental programs produce persistent astrocyte and vascular identity differences; and (iv) postnatal cell migration, as documented for the perivascular migration of oligodendrocyte precursor cells [25,26]. In the corrected candidate screen, mechanism (ii) is most directly implicated by the candidate composition and mechanism (iv) the least: only 2 of 39 Strong candidates are OPC or committed OPC, and the oligodendrocyte lineage as a whole is not enriched (fold 0.77, P = 0.92), so migration-driven similarity should be read as a possibility the data do not currently evidence. Mechanism assignment below is therefore a literature-guided interpretation, not a validated discovery. Region abbreviations follow the Siletti et al. dissection nomenclature (glossary in Additional file 1: Note 3.14); the numbered labels (A13, A14, A38, A40, A46, etc.) denote Brodmann-designated cortical areas, not thalamic nuclei, and TF denotes the temporal area TF of the occipitotemporal (fusiform) gyrus.')

p('To formalize the screen, we used a multiplicative model: for each (cell_type, region_pair) combination, expected_\u03c9 = \u03bc_ct \u00d7 \u03bc_pair / \u03bc_grand, where \u03bc_ct is the cell type\u2019s global mean \u03c9, \u03bc_pair the region pair\u2019s mean \u03c9, and \u03bc_grand the global mean (38.55). The multiplicative residual = observed / expected: a residual substantially below 1 indicates the cell type is far less differentiated between two regions than expected from both its own global plasticity and the pair\u2019s overall divergence\u2014a signature of shared transcriptional state potentially reflecting shared developmental history or recent migration. Three confidence tiers were defined: Strong (residual < 0.3, \u03c9 < 15, lowest \u03c9 in the region pair), Moderate (residual < 0.5, \u03c9 < 25), and Weak (residual < 0.75, \u03c9 < 35; Additional file 1: Fig. S8).')

p(f'Among {_br["total_pairs"]:,} cross-region comparisons, threshold criteria identified {_br["n_strong"]} ({_br["pct_strong"]:.2f}%) Strong candidates, with {_br["n_moderate"]:,} ({_br["pct_moderate"]:.2f}%) Moderate and {_br["n_weak"]:,} ({_br["pct_weak"]:.2f}%) Weak (Additional file 1: Table S4). Under the block-shuffle null (B = 1,000; Additional file 1: Fig. S9), 31 of the 39 Strong candidates showed raw one-sided P < 0.05, but after Benjamini-Hochberg correction across all 31,764 pairs none reached q < 0.05 (minimum q = 0.520). A selection-rule empirical FDR analysis, recomputing the full tier rule on each permutation, quantifies this directly: the null expectation of the Strong rule is 148.3 candidates, 3.8-fold above the 39 observed (P(null count \u2265 39) = 1.0), and for the Strong rule restricted to raw P < 0.05 the null expectation (131.2) exceeds the observed 31 4.2-fold. The rule is therefore anti-enriched relative to the null at every level. A cell-type-stratified BH sensitivity analysis yields a single family below the nominal threshold\u2014the Bergmann-glia family (m = 21 pairs; minimum q = 0.042, 4 pairs at q < 0.05)\u2014while the remaining nine families have minimum q \u2265 0.28. Because all Bergmann-glia comparisons are intra-cerebellar, no candidate crosses class boundaries under stratification. Globally, 1,960 of 31,764 pairs (6.2%) showed raw lower-tail P < 0.05, above the null mean of 1,588 and its 99th percentile (1,956; P = 0.011, 0.022 after Bonferroni correction across the two tails). The excess is contributed chiefly by classes whose mean \u03c9 sits below the null expectation (microglia: 8.06% of class pairs at lower-tail P < 0.05, +173 above the 5% null rate; oligodendrocytes: 6.01%, +58; Bergmann glia: 8 of 21 pairs, 38.1%), but also by astrocytes (7.60%, +150), whose wide right-skewed \u03c9 distributions generate low-\u03c9 pairs despite a high class mean. The complementary upper tail shows the opposite excess: 2,510 pairs (7.9%) had upper-tail P < 0.05, concentrated in the most regionally structured classes (astrocytes 16.2%, committed OPCs 9.7%, OPCs 9.6%; Bergmann glia 0%). The per-pair P-value distribution is thus shifted, consistent with the class-level structure (4 of 10 classes above the null, 3 below), reflecting distributional shifts rather than individually validated low-\u03c9 pairs. We therefore present the Strong candidates as a prioritized hypothesis list. Candidates concentrate in microglia: 16 Strong candidates (13 with raw P < 0.05), versus oligodendrocytes 10 (10), fibroblasts 6 (4), astrocytes 3 (3), ependymal cells 2 (0), committed OPCs 1 (0), and OPCs 1 (1); vascular cells, choroid plexus, and Bergmann glia contributed none. Across a grid of 20 (residual-cap, \u03c9-cap) combinations spanning residual < 0.2\u20130.4 and \u03c9 < 12\u201325, no combination showed oligodendrocyte-lineage enrichment (fold 0\u20131.15, hypergeometric P \u2265 0.43 at every grid point). The microglial concentration, measured against the class share of comparisons, appears as a 2.30-fold enrichment (hypergeometric P = 6.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074; Bonferroni-corrected P = 0.006), but the comparison share is not the relevant null: the Strong rule itself fires preferentially on low-\u03c9 classes\u2014of the 148.3 null-rule candidates, 52.0 (35%) are microglial, an intrinsic 1.96-fold bias. Against this design-matched null, microglia show no enrichment (observed 16 versus 52.0 expected; fold 0.31; P(null microglial count \u2265 16) = 0.990). The concentration does not survive the alternative gene-selection schemes (leave-pair-out panel: 85 of 457 Strong candidates, a 1.04-fold share-level enrichment with hypergeometric P = 0.36; all-gene panel: 1 of 10), and across the same 20-combination grid the share-level enrichment is Bonferroni-significant at 11 of 20 threshold combinations. The apparent concentration is therefore a property of the rule interacting with the class-\u03c9 scale, and we make no class-composition claim; it also reconciles the class-level test, where microglia\u2019s mean \u03c9 sits below the null expectation (P = 0.904); both reflect the same downward shift of the microglial \u03c9 distribution.')

p('A design-matched negative control confirms that the block-shuffle null is calibrated by library-level structure rather than by the regional structure it is designed to test. For each cell type, the libraries of every region were split uniformly at random into two pseudo-regions, and the identical block-shuffle test was re-run on the resulting 127,756 pseudo-pairs (B = 1,000; same gene model, filters, and P-value formulae; Additional file 1: Note 4.6; Additional file 1: Fig. S14). Pseudo-pairs formed between halves of different real regions (n = 127,056)\u2014the direct analogue of the real cross-region pairs\u2014gave near-nominal marginal tail rates (5.79% lower tail and 6.87% upper tail against the 5% nominal level), similar in direction and magnitude to the real analysis (6.17% and 7.90%), whereas pairs formed between the two halves of the same region (n = 700) showed a 37.6% lower-tail rate. Because the random split destroys regional structure but preserves within-region library grouping (donor, dissection, and batch effects tied to each region), the closeness of the pseudo-pair and real-pair marginal rates demonstrates that the mild over-dispersion of the per-pair P-values (visible as a slight S-shaped deviation in the QQ plots, Additional file 1: Fig. S14) arises from library-level grouping shared by both designs, not from a null-width mismatch driven by real regional structure; and the 37.6% same-origin rate confirms that the test retains full power to detect within-region library similarity where it exists. The small excess of the real over the pseudo marginal rates (0.4 and 1.0 percentage points in the lower and upper tails) is the marginal signature of the true regional biology reported above.')

heading('Microglia: composition of the largest candidate share', level=3)
p('Microglia contributed the largest share of Strong candidates (16 of 39, of which 13 had raw block-shuffle P < 0.05), a nominal 2.3-fold share-level enrichment (17.9% of pairs; nominal hypergeometric P = 6.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074). As quantified above, this share-level excess is an artifact of the screening rule\u2019s intrinsic bias toward low-\u03c9 classes (fold 0.31 against the null-rule expectation; Results), and the concentration does not survive the leave-pair-out gene-panel scheme, so we make no class-composition claim from it. The microglial candidates nonetheless show convergences worth cataloguing: five pairs involve the lateral geniculate nucleus (LG) as one endpoint (Cla-LG, BM-LG, A14-LG, LG-TF, A13-LG), four involve the pulvinar (Pul; Cla-Pul, A14-Pul, A13-Pul, Pul-TF), and four the occipitotemporal area TF (the temporal part of the fusiform gyrus; LG-TF, DTg-TF, Pul-TF, PnAN-TF), with the orbitofrontal cortical areas A13 (caudal intermediate orbital gyrus) and A14 (gyrus rectus, medial orbitofrontal cortex) appearing as endpoints in three pairs each. Because no candidate survives FDR correction and the concentration mirrors the rule\u2019s bias, we read these entries as hypothesis-generating targets for spatial-transcriptomic and lineage follow-up [21]\u2014consistent with the class-level observation that the microglial mean \u03c9 sits below the null expectation (P = 0.904)\u2014not as evidence of microglia-specific regional association.')

heading('Oligodendrocytes: thalamo-temporal convergence', level=3)
p('Mature oligodendrocytes contributed 10 Strong candidates, all with raw P < 0.05\u2014the only class in which every Strong candidate reaches nominal significance. Four pairs involve the occipitotemporal area TF as one endpoint (GPe-TF, MG-TF, LP-TF, STH-TF), three the medial geniculate nucleus (MG; A13-MG, MG-Pro, MG-TF), and six of the ten have a thalamic relay-nucleus endpoint (LP, Pul, VA, or MG; eight if the subthalamic nucleus STH, which the atlas dissection ontology groups under thalamus but is not a thalamic relay nucleus, is also counted)\u2014a notable convergence given the 108 regions available to this class, tested post hoc at the set level under both a uniform-draw and a selection-rule-matched null (Discussion; Additional file 1: Note 5.1). Committed oligodendrocyte precursors contributed a single Strong candidate (AON-PnRF, raw P = 0.097), and OPCs one (MD-Re-Pul, raw P = 0.001). Adult oligodendrocytes are generally considered non-migratory between regions; Foerster et al. [27] demonstrated that cortical oligodendrocytes are predominantly dorsally derived, whereas thalamic and brainstem oligodendrocytes are ventrally derived, with persistent cell-autonomous transcriptional identity. The thalamo-temporal orientation of these candidates\u2014low-\u03c9 pairs linking thalamic (ventral-origin) dissections with the occipitotemporal area TF (dorsal/cortical-origin), i.e., crossing rather than following dorsoventral origin boundaries\u2014cannot be attributed to migration and is not straightforwardly predicted by the origin-boundary model either; we therefore leave its mechanistic basis unassigned and treat the anatomical concentration itself as the observation to be explained. Given the null FDR result, we present this as one candidate interpretation among several.')

heading('Astrocytes, fibroblasts, and ependymal cells: sparse candidates', level=3)
p('Astrocytes, the most regionally divergent class (mean \u03c9 = 82.75), contributed only 3 Strong candidates (A43-A5/A7, CBL-CBV, CM-Pf-VPL, all with raw P < 0.05); fibroblasts contributed 6, of which 5 involve area 40 as one endpoint (A40-MD, A40-PnRF, A40-IC, A40-PN, PAG-DR-A40; together with PAG-DR-SI; 4 of 6 with raw P < 0.05), and ependymal cells contributed 2 (CA1C-CA3C-Pul and NAC-Pul, both raw P > 0.05). Bergmann glia, vascular cells, and choroid plexus contributed none; the absence of Bergmann glia candidates is consistent with their developmentally patterned, transcriptionally constrained state in the adult cerebellum [28,29] and their low global \u03c9 (13.56)\u2014though all Bergmann glia comparisons are intra-cerebellar, so both the low class mean and the absence of candidates partly reflect the anatomical restriction of this class to a single brain structure. None of these sparse signals survive multiple-testing correction; they are catalogued because their region identities are compatible with published developmental mechanisms (compartmentalized astrogenesis [30]; cerebellar molecular topography [28]), making them suitable targets for targeted validation.')

p('In summary, the block-shuffle re-analysis substantially tempers the brain candidate catalogue: the cell-class-level differentiation gradient is supported for 4 of 10 classes (3 of 10 after Benjamini-Hochberg correction), with microglia, oligodendrocytes, and Bergmann glia showing observed means below the null expectation, whereas no individual cell-type/region-pair candidate survives FDR correction (minimum q = 0.520; the cell-type-stratified minimum q = 0.042 is confined to the intra-cerebellar Bergmann-glia family). An earlier implementation of this test, which shuffled cell-type labels within each region pair (B = 10,000), produced anti-conservative P-values (36.3% of pairs at the P-value floor) because per-pair shuffling ignores the block structure of 10x libraries; the block-shuffle null used here preserves that structure. We therefore position the entire candidate list as hypothesis-generating, prioritized for lineage-tracing and spatial-transcriptomic validation rather than presented as discoveries.')


# ============================================================
# DISCUSSION (NAR: can merge Results and Discussion, but we keep separate)
# ============================================================
heading('Discussion', level=1)

p('CKI reframes transcriptomic comparison\u2014from measuring absolute distance to quantifying functional divergence relative to an internal baseline\u2014but this is better described as a recombination of established ideas than a conceptual shift. Reference genes have long anchored normalization and quality control in expression measurement: qPCR normalization by geometric averaging of validated internal control genes [31], and the constrained-variance property of housekeeping genes has been characterized at the genomic level [13,32]. The idea that an intrinsic, gene-property-dependent noise floor can serve as a baseline has likewise been central to the transcriptional-noise literature [33-35], which showed that expression variance is reproducibly gene-specific. CKI\u2019s specific contribution is to combine these ingredients\u2014a constrained reference set, a functional panel, and their divergence ratio\u2014into a per-comparison, design-testable index with an explicit split-half calibration and a design-matched permutation null, rather than to introduce a new concept. The key assumption is that housekeeping (HK) genes are under stabilizing selection that constrains their expression variance across conditions, making them a practical constrained baseline against which functional divergence can be measured. This decomposition is heuristically inspired by Ka/Ks analysis, but CKI is a heuristic index rather than a formal measure of selection: unlike Ka/Ks\u2014where a shared mutation rate cancels mathematically, leaving a pure selection signal\u2014CKI uses empirically defined HK genes as the baseline, lacking a comparable mechanistic cancellation. The empirical calibration (split-half equivalent populations, n = 6) yielded a mean \u03c9 = 6.67 (95% bootstrap CI [4.24, 9.24]), indicating that k_f systematically exceeds k_n even for identical populations due to identity-gene selection. We therefore use \u03c9_cal = \u03c9 / 6.67 as the operational scale, interpreting \u03c9_cal relative to the empirical equivalent-population distribution rather than against fixed cut-offs: values well below the calibrated baseline suggest functional constraint and values well above it enhanced divergence\u2014not as claims about Darwinian selection regimes, and with the caveat that the baseline is dataset-dependent (Results).')

p('CKI is a divergence index, not a classifier\u2014and this is by design. Classifying cell types from transcriptomic data is largely a solved problem; CKI answers a complementary question: regardless of cell-type labels, how much functional divergence separates two populations, relative to their shared baseline? The negative correlation with all standard metrics initially suggested complementary information content, but the decomposition analysis showed this association to be partly a denominator effect (k_n is itself strongly positively correlated with the standard metrics), so \u03c9 should be understood as a composite of numerator and denominator information rather than a fully independent signal dimension.')

p('The Ka/Ks analogy is heuristically productive but has technical limitations that distinguish CKI from formal molecular evolution models. Ka/Ks operates on DNA sequence alignments with explicit codon models, whereas CKI uses continuous expression vectors without a substitution model; HK genes are defined empirically (high detection rate, low CV) rather than mechanistically (synonymous sites in Ka/Ks), and their expression variance could reflect regulatory constraints rather than pure neutral drift\u2014sensitivity analysis with alternative low-variance gene sets (r > 0.95) partially mitigates this concern. CKI also lacks a formal phylogenetic framework (e.g., Ornstein-Uhlenbeck models), and \u03c9 is computed on different gene sets per comparison, limiting absolute cross-comparison interpretability: users should compare \u03c9 ranks rather than absolute values across datasets.')

p('CKI complements rather than replaces existing methods. SAMap [36] and SATURN [4] excel at cross-species alignment; CACIMAR [37] provides conservation scoring that could be reinterpreted through the CKI lens. However, we did not quantitatively benchmark CKI against these specialized methods, as they address different questions (cross-species alignment vs. within-species functional divergence). A systematic comparison on shared datasets would clarify the complementary strengths of each approach. More broadly, CKI provides a principled null model for any transcriptomic comparison: before concluding that two populations are meaningfully different, ask whether the difference exceeds baseline expectation.')

p('The TCGA finding that tumors are more transcriptionally homogeneous than normal tissues (median NN/TT > 1.0) is an exploratory observation requiring cautious interpretation: at bulk RNA-seq resolution the apparent convergence could be driven by cell-composition shifts (tumor purity, stromal and immune infiltration), peritumoral inflammation, or systematic RNA quality differences rather than genuine transcriptional convergence of tumor cells. Component decomposition reinforces this caution: the NN/TT reversal is predominantly a k_n effect\u2014normal-normal pairs show systematically lower housekeeping-gene divergence than tumor-tumor pairs in all five cancer types, while tumor pairs show the higher functional-gene divergence (k_f)\u2014so the data do not indicate smaller functional divergence of tumor cells. A marker-panel composition check quantifies the composition confound directly (Additional file 1: Note 5.2): tumor-tumor pairs do show larger composition differences than normal-normal pairs (median |Delta z| 1.33\u20131.46-fold higher across the immune, stromal, and epithelial panels, and 1.24-fold with the myeloid panel included; all P < 10\u207b\u2077\u2077), and within tumor-tumor pairs k_n correlates with the overall four-panel composition difference (Spearman \u03c1 = 0.387 pooled; 0.23\u20130.52 per cancer type); however, in regressions of log k_n on pair type with the four composition deltas as covariates\u2014including a myeloid panel (CD68, CD163, LST1, FCGR3A, C1QA)\u2014and sample-level cluster bootstrap confidence intervals (B = 200), the tumor-pair coefficient attenuates by \u22120.5% pooled (95% CI [\u22123.2%, +2.6%]), with marked per-cancer heterogeneity (BRCA \u221214.0%, LUSC \u22129.7%, LUAD \u22122.3%, KIRC +19.6%, LIHC +33.5%). Because marker panels are noisy proxies for true composition, these attenuation estimates are lower bounds on the true composition contribution; the pooled tumor-pair k_n excess is therefore not fully explained by a noisy marker-panel proxy, but neither can a marker-panel regression exclude composition as the driver\u2014plausibly reflecting tumor-specific housekeeping dysregulation [38] or RNA-quality differences instead, and single-cell or deconvolution-based validation remains necessary. The PAM50 gradient cannot be read as a functional-divergence ordering: under k_f-only analysis the subtype ordering largely reverses (Luminal A lowest mean k_f, Basal-like highest; Additional file 1: Note 3.16), so the \u03c9 gradient is predominantly a baseline effect, and the proliferation-confound explanation (aggressive subtypes proliferate fastest) remains; single-cell or deconvolution-based validation would be needed. The raw \u03c9 scale also varies substantially across datasets and computation schemes (e.g., BRCA Luminal A \u03c9 \u2248 123.4 versus mean brain regional \u03c9 = 38.55). In TCGA, per-pair k_n is comparable to the single-cell datasets (mean 0.005, range 1.6 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074\u20135.6 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b2, versus a mean per-pair k_n of 0.0035 in the brain single-nucleus dataset), so the higher raw \u03c9 in TCGA is driven primarily by a larger k_f: bulk tumor-versus-normal expression differences span thousands of genes. Cross-dataset \u03c9 comparisons should therefore be interpreted as rank-based rather than absolute.')

p('The cross-organ and cross-brain-region analyses indicate that CKI can serve as a general tool for measuring functional differentiation at multiple spatial scales. The brain analysis showed a 6.10-fold \u03c9 gradient across 10 cell classes (from 13.56 in Bergmann glia and vascular cells to 82.75 in astrocytes; excluding Bergmann glia, whose comparisons are all intra-cerebellar, the gradient remains 6.10-fold; the ratio of class means is invariant to any shared calibration constant, and under class-specific split-half baselines it is 5.99, 95% CI [4.12, 9.18] under the joint region-clustered bootstrap), significant at the cell-class level under a block-shuffle null for 4 of 10 classes (3 of 10 after Benjamini-Hochberg correction). Component decomposition, however, shows the gradient is a composite signal dominated by the k_n denominator (k_n contributes 3.21-fold versus 2.03-fold from k_f; product 6.51, slightly above the 6.10 ratio of class means because the mean of per-pair ratios is not the ratio of component means), so it should not be read as a pure functional-divergence ranking. The multiplicative residual model identified 39 Strong candidates; under the block-shuffle null (B = 1,000) 31 showed raw P < 0.05, but none survived Benjamini-Hochberg correction across all 31,764 pairs (minimum q = 0.520); the raw-P enrichment is strongly tier-dependent (raw P < 0.05 in 79.5% of Strong, 35.6% of Moderate, 16.9% of Weak, and 2.4% of unclassified pairs, versus 6.2% overall), which we report as a post-hoc coherence check\u2014its statistical caveats, including the mathematical coupling between the tier rule and the per-pair P values (both are monotone functions of the same observed and null \u03c9), are quantified in Additional file 1: Note 5.1\u2014rather than FDR-controlled evidence; an earlier per-pair shuffle implementation was anti-conservative (36.3% of pairs at the P-value floor) because it ignored the experimental block structure, which the block-shuffle null rectifies. The candidates concentrate in microglia (16 of 39) and mature oligodendrocytes (10, all with raw P < 0.05), while the oligodendrocyte lineage as a whole shows no enrichment (12 of 39 candidates versus a 40.2% share of comparisons; fold enrichment 0.77, P = 0.92). Re-analysis against the null-rule composition shows the microglial concentration mirrors the screening rule\u2019s intrinsic bias toward low-\u03c9 classes (fold 0.31; Results). The thalamo-temporal orientation of the oligodendrocyte candidates with the developmental origin boundaries of mature oligodendrocytes [27] remains an interpretation requiring lineage-tracing validation. These results illustrate a general principle for high-throughput screens in single-cell atlases: statistical claims require nulls that respect the experimental design, and the absence of FDR-significant signals is itself informative, delimiting what can be concluded from adult transcriptomes alone. Preliminary cross-species validation using shared cell types between mouse and human atlases [39] was limited by the small number of directly comparable cell types: across the 15 shared cell types, per-cell-type mean \u03c9 rankings showed no significant cross-species correlation (Spearman r = \u22120.17, P = 0.55; Additional file 1: Fig. S10), so cell-type-level cross-species transferability of \u03c9 remains unestablished; absolute values also differ with the computation scheme (global top-2,000-HVG full matrix for mouse, per-pair hybrid for human).')

p('Although no individual candidate survives FDR correction, several signals are consistent with prior reports\u2014literature anchoring rather than independent validation. Maturation comparisons must be made on the k_f-only ordering rather than the composite \u03c9 ordering, because the composite is k_n-dominated (Results): under k_f alone the lineage ranks committed OPCs (k_f class mean 0.197, the highest of all ten classes; estimated from only 4,118 nuclei and 1,326 pairs, the sparsest stage of the lineage, so this rank carries the widest sampling uncertainty of the three) above oligodendrocytes (0.054), with OPCs lowest (0.048)\u2014an ordering partially consistent with the single-cell observation that early oligodendrocyte differentiation stages are broadly similar across juvenile CNS regions, whereas subsets of mature oligodendrocytes become enriched in specific regions of the adult brain [40,41], although the high k_f rank of committed OPCs (highest of all ten classes) is predicted by neither reference\u2014whereas the composite \u03c9 ordering gives the opposite picture (OPCs 40.62 above oligodendrocytes 37.05 above committed OPCs 28.84). The tension between the two orderings is itself informative: neither supports the simple monotone-maturation reading of the composite gradient. The thalamo-temporal orientation of the mature-oligodendrocyte candidates (GPe\u2013TF, MG\u2013TF, LP\u2013TF, STH\u2013TF, with MG appearing in three pairs and six of ten candidates having a thalamic relay-nucleus endpoint, eight if the subthalamic nucleus is counted) concentrates on the thalamo-temporal axis rather than being scattered across the 108 regions. We tested this concentration two ways. A uniform-draw permutation test that preserves the endpoint co-occurrence structure of the 5,778 mature-oligodendrocyte pairs (10 pairs drawn without replacement, B = 100,000) gives thalamic-relay endpoint in 6 of 10 candidates versus a null mean of 1.95, P = 1.005 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2075; temporal-fusiform endpoint in 4 of 10 versus a null mean of 0.19; the combined thalamo-temporal axis in 9 of 10 versus a null mean of 2.27. A selection-rule-matched null at the same specification as the microglia composition analysis\u2014the Strong rule (low \u03c9, high multiplicative deviation, lowest-in-pair) re-evaluated on every block-shuffle permutation (B = 1,000)\u2014qualifies this: the rule-matched null generates more survivors than observed (mean 43.7 versus 10), so absolute hit counts are not extreme (thalamic-relay 6 versus a null mean of 6.58, P = 0.48; temporal-fusiform 4 versus 1.51, P = 0.13; combined axis 9 versus 8.49, P = 0.38), whereas the per-candidate hit rate remains concentrated across all three endpoints (thalamic-relay 0.60 versus a null mean rate of 0.15, P = 0.005; temporal-fusiform 0.40 versus 0.03, P = 0.002; combined axis 0.90 versus 0.19, P = 0.001; Additional file 1: Note 5.1). We therefore claim axis concentration of the surviving candidates, not an axis excess of candidates\u2014a pattern difficult to reconcile with migration; its correspondence with the developmental organization of forebrain oligodendrocytes demonstrated experimentally by Foerster et al. [27] remains at the level of axis anatomy, however, because the candidate pairs cross rather than follow the dorsoventral origin boundary. Each of these correspondences is a consistency check at the level of direction and anatomical logic; none constitutes independent validation of any specific region pair, and lineage-tracing or spatial-transcriptomic follow-up remains necessary.')

heading('When to use CKI versus standard metrics', level=2)
p('CKI and standard metrics answer different questions, and choosing between them can be reduced to a few practical rules. Use CKI when the question is relative: is the functional divergence between two populations larger than what their own housekeeping baseline varies by, within the same dataset and gene-selection scheme? CKI then offers (i) a normalized, interpretable scale (\u03c9 relative to the split-half calibration), (ii) higher discrimination than raw distances for strong perturbations (simulation AUC 0.80 for \u03c9 versus 0.72 for k_f, 0.64 for raw JS, and 0.58 for cosine\u2014under the simulation\u2019s HK-anchored neutral-drift definition and top-200 gene selection; Results), and (iii) a specificity-first screen: with a design-matched permutation null, a significant \u03c9 rejects label exchangeability under that null, and attribution to functional divergence further requires excluding library-level confounders (batch, donor, dissection order, RNA quality; Limitations); drift on the anchor is the null hypothesis by construction, and functional signal on the anchor is invisible to \u03c9. Before trusting fine-grained \u03c9 orderings, diagnose the denominator: if per-pair k_n itself lies in the extreme tail of its own null distribution, or k_n correlates with technical covariates (composition estimates, batch, RNA-quality proxies), report k_f (or SES(k_f)) with the design-matched null directly rather than the ratio. In the real data analyzed here, \u03c9 orderings were predominantly denominator-driven (brain class-mean \u03c1(\u03c9, k_n) = \u22120.73; TCGA NN/TT inversion and severity gradients predominantly denominator effects; cross-organ cell-type ranking not reproduced under k_f-only; Additional file 1: Note 3.16), so the honest default for ordering claims is k_f plus a design-matched null, with \u03c9 reserved for its specificity-first property\u2014rejecting HK-anchored neutral drift\u2014rather than for ranking. A real perturbation demonstration (Results) supports this boundary within the limits of its lane-confounded design: IFN-\u03b2 stimulation raises k_n itself 1.2\u20135.7-fold above the donor-drift level, and where it does so most strongly (CD14+ monocytes) the \u03c9 AUC for separating perturbation from donor drift falls to 0.55 while k_f retains 0.98. Use standard metrics when any of the following hold: absolute distances with cross-dataset meaning are required (\u03c9 does not transfer across datasets or schemes\u2014compare ranks only); the task is classification or clustering of cell types (a largely solved problem where normalization adds little); weak-to-moderate effects must be detected at atlas scale (CKI\u2019s sensitivity is bounded by permutation resolution and multiplicity, as the brain analysis illustrates); or the analysis needs an orthogonal signal dimension (\u03c9 is a k_n-dominated composite correlated with the standard metrics, not an independent axis). When the baseline itself is suspect\u2014bulk data with composition shifts, as in TCGA\u2014interpret k_n and k_f separately (or use k_f-only orderings with composition controls) rather than trusting \u03c9 alone.')

heading('Practical usage guide', level=2)
p('For prospective applications of CKI we recommend the following. (i) Cell numbers: use at least 100\u2013200 cells per group; the calibration controls and simulation replicates used 100\u2013200 cells, and below this range pseudobulk noise inflates both k_n and k_f unpredictably. (ii) Reference gene set: use the HRT Atlas v1.0 housekeeping panel (shipped with the package as cki/data/hrt_atlas.csv) rather than data-driven auto-detection, so that k_n is anchored to a pre-specified constrained gene set. (iii) Estimation: compare populations only within the same dataset and the same gene-selection scheme; absolute \u03c9 does not transfer across datasets or schemes (Results). (iv) Baseline: calibrate within the dataset of interest by randomly splitting the same population into halves and computing split-half \u03c9 with the analysis pipeline being used, and interpret \u03c9 relative to that internal baseline rather than the mouse-derived 6.67. (v) Inference: use a permutation null that matches the experimental design (block-shuffle where libraries or donors define blocks)\u2014the reference implementation used here is provided in the package as cki.blocknull.block_shuffle_test\u2014and expect bounded sensitivity: \u03c9 is a specificity-first screen whose value is rejecting neutral drift, not detecting weak-to-moderate functional shifts.')

heading('Limitations', level=2)

p('Scope of the index, parameters, and test direction. CKI currently operates at the pseudobulk level; single-cell extensions would need to address sparsity and dropout. Key parameters (the +1 normalization pseudo-count, the \u03b5 = 1e-9 numerical guard, top-200 differentially expressed (DE) genes for k_f, HVG count of 2,000, log-base 2 for JS divergence) were chosen based on practical considerations rather than formal optimization; sensitivity analyses showed robustness to parameter variation, but a systematic simultaneous parameter sweep was not performed. The \u03c9 distribution is right-skewed and non-normal for the large datasets (brain skewness = 2.22, excess kurtosis = 6.02, D\u2019Agostino-Pearson P < 2.2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u2076; human Shapiro-Wilk P = 7.6 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074\u00b2; the mouse pilot at n = 15 pairs does not reject normality, P = 0.071; Additional file 1: Fig. S11), so SES is reported as a descriptive measure of effect size rather than a parametric test statistic. The one-sided permutation test (H1: \u03c9_obs > \u03c9_null) does not detect functional constraint (\u03c9_obs < \u03c9_null); users investigating bidirectional hypotheses should employ two-sided permutation tests, available via the direction parameter in the CKI package. The brain analysis was restricted to non-neuronal cell types because the supercluster_term annotation does not resolve neuronal subtype heterogeneity, limiting the generalizability of our brain regional findings to non-neuronal lineages.')

p('Gene-set definition, calibration, and estimator dependence. CKI uses the HRT Atlas v1.0 reference [13] for all reported analyses; the package also supports data-driven auto-detection (combined detection-rate and CV filtering, use_reference = False), not used here, and sensitivity analysis showed robustness to alternative HK definitions (the lowest 10% variable genes as a constrained set yielded \u03c9 correlations r > 0.95). Identity-gene selection for k_f introduces systematic inflation: calibration controls (random split of the same population) yielded a mean \u03c9 = 6.67, because pre-selecting genes by the observed group difference (DE scheme) or by high cross-cell variance (HVG scheme) inflates k_f even for identical populations; the permutation test accounts for this by constructing the null under the same gene-selection procedure. A further concern is the circular dependency of the per-pair k_f scheme\u2014the top-200 genes are selected by |\u03bc_A \u2212 \u03bc_B|, so the genes defining functional divergence are precisely those with the largest expression differences\u2014and although the permutation null preserves this selection, k_f magnitudes lack independent external validation and should be interpreted as an upper bound; the fixed gene-panel ablation (Results) confirmed that replacing the circular panel deflates absolute \u03c9 while preserving rankings, so absolute tier thresholds are scheme-specific. Housekeeping gene expression may be dysregulated in cancer [38], potentially affecting the k_n baseline in a disease-specific manner\u2014one instance of the anchor-failure scope stated with the concept definition (Background). The empirical calibration factor (\u03c9_cal = \u03c9 / 6.67, 95% bootstrap CI [4.24, 9.24]) derives from only n = 6 mouse split-half controls under the per-pair DE hybrid scheme; the mouse full pairwise matrix (703 pairs, Additional file 1: Fig. S1) uses a different scheme (global top-2,000 HVGs), so cross-scheme comparison of absolute \u03c9 requires caution. Applying the CI bounds shifts \u03c9_cal by factors of 1.57 and 0.72; all CKI conclusions, which rely on rank-based interpretation, are robust to this range. Scheme-matched split-half calibration inside the two large single-cell datasets shows the baseline is dataset-dependent: the Tabula Sapiens internal baseline is 7.67 (95% bootstrap CI [7.39, 8.00]) and lies within the mouse CI [4.24, 9.24], whereas the brain-internal baseline (9.73, 95% CI [9.03, 10.53]) is approximately 1.5-fold higher, so the mouse-derived \u03c9_cal overstates calibrated values in the brain dataset (Bergmann glia \u03c9_cal \u2248 1.4 under the internal baseline rather than \u2248 2). \u03c9_cal therefore restores the interpretability of the ratio framework only in a dataset-relative sense, and the baseline may vary across tissues, species, and platforms. We verified scheme-specific transferability directly through scheme-matched split-half calibration inside the brain atlas and inside Tabula Sapiens (Methods); dataset-internal baselines\u2014computable with the split-half procedure and calibrate_omega() in the CKI package\u2014should be preferred whenever absolute calibrated values are required. Although k_n is computed per pair on the shared HK gene set, it varies substantially across pairs (brain per-pair CV = 97.52%), and the class-level \u03c9 gradient is predominantly denominator-driven (Results); a global-k_n variant produced substantially different \u03c9 rankings because it effectively rescales k_f alone, whereas an aggregate-first estimator preserved the ordering (Results), so fine-grained \u03c9 orderings should be interpreted with the estimator choice in mind. Finally, k_n can approach zero under strong pseudobulk averaging (e.g., bulk RNA-seq); users comparing absolute \u03c9 magnitudes across datasets should verify that per-pair k_n is well above any floor applied (TCGA applies kn_floor = 1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074; all single-cell analyses use the package default kn_floor = 0; Methods).')

p('Data- and design-specific constraints. TCGA analysis was limited to bulk RNA-seq resolution; the per-cancer permutation test (B = 1,000) permutes sample labels (tumor vs. normal) and holds the identity gene panel fixed across permutations, which is anti-conservative relative to per-permutation re-selection (Methods), and with 5 cancer types the TCGA results are exploratory. On the pair-level TCGA data (35,306 pairs) the kn_floor = 1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074 denominator floor was never triggered (0 pairs below it; minimum k_n = 1.62 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074), so pair-level \u03c9 equals k_f/k_n exactly and is a k_n-dominated composite (Spearman \u03c1(\u03c9, k_f) = \u22120.10; log-scale variance of k_n accounts for 78%); the saturation caveat applies only to the aggregate tumor-versus-normal analysis (aggregate k_n ranged from 3.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2075 to 1.9 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074, so aggregate \u03c9 saturates at k_f/10\u207b\u2074 in 3 of 5 cancer types), and all single-cell analyses use the package default positivity guard only (kn_floor = 0), leaving k_n uncapped. The brain analysis uses post-mortem tissue; post-mortem interval (PMI) varies across samples and may differentially affect RNA integrity by region and cell type, potentially biasing regional \u03c9 comparisons\u2014in particular, mitochondrial transcripts degrade faster post-mortem than nuclear-encoded mRNAs, so regionally differential degradation of mitochondrial and nuclear-encoded metabolic genes within the HK set could masquerade as regional structure in k_n; because the candidate screen is rule-driven rather than evidence of PMI robustness (Results), PMI sensitivity would need per-candidate evaluation\u2014particularly for the thalamic and cortical dissections\u2014and developmental time courses with controlled collection would provide stronger evidence for region-association inference. Donor-level PMI and RNA-integrity covariates were not available to us for this atlas, so a direct correlation between PMI and per-pair k_n could not be tested; such a check is the minimal sensitivity analysis required before any candidate-level region-association claim. In addition, the brain atlas is single-nucleus rather than whole-cell RNA-seq: nuclear capture depletes cytoplasmic transcripts, and if this depletion acts differently on housekeeping (k_n) and identity (k_f) gene sets\u2014for example, because identity genes are enriched for secreted or membrane proteins\u2014per-pair \u03c9 could shift systematically across classes; we did not model this nucleocytoplasmic bias explicitly. Similarly, the +1 pseudocount added before the JS divergence acts asymmetrically on the two gene sets: k_n, computed on highly expressed housekeeping genes, is largely insensitive to it, whereas k_f, which includes lowly expressed regulatory genes, is more affected, and JS divergence on probability vectors is inherently dominated by high-expression genes (Additional file 1: Note 3.6 excludes dimension effects but not expression-level effects); the \u2018functional\u2019 reading of k_f should be tempered accordingly. A semi-synthetic ground-truth simulation (module-shift injection on a real single-cell background; Additional file 1: Note 3.12) validated the specificity of \u03c9 against neutral drift (type-I error 0.00\u20130.02, versus 0.55\u20130.58 for raw JS and cosine) but bounded its sensitivity: detecting injected module shifts was background-dependent\u2014in the marrow background \u03c9 required \u03b4 = 2 (fourfold shifts on at least 200 genes), whereas the skin background reached 91% detection at \u03b4 = 1 (twofold shifts on at least 200 genes)\u2014and \u03c9 ranked below raw JS and cosine in raw detection power while ranking first in functional-versus-neutral discrimination (AUC = 0.80). The simulation was restricted to two backgrounds (one cell type each: marrow B cells and skin keratinocyte stem cells), a single module-size series, and simple multiplicative perturbations; lineage-structured, gradual, or compensatory ground truths were not evaluated, and no head-to-head comparison against specialized cross-dataset integration methods (e.g., SATURN) was performed.')

p('Multiple testing and cluster-aware inference. The brain regional analysis generated 31,764 cross-region comparisons; Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction was applied within each dataset separately (group-level tests: m = 10 for brain, 17 for human, 15 for mouse; residual model: m = 31,764). The class-level upper-tail tests (m = 10) and the pair-level lower-tail screen (m = 31,764) are families with opposite directional hypotheses and are reported separately; no cross-family joint correction is applied, so a rejection in one family licenses no claim in the other. The 39 Strong candidates represent the most extreme deviations from the multiplicative expectation; none survived Benjamini-Hochberg correction under the block-shuffle null (minimum q = 0.520; the cell-type-stratified minimum q = 0.042 is confined to the intra-cerebellar Bergmann-glia family and crosses no class boundaries), and 31 had raw P < 0.05\u2014they should be interpreted as hypothesis-generating signals requiring independent validation, with the systematic concordance with known developmental biology providing post hoc plausibility but not FDR-controlled discovery. The block-shuffle null treats each 10x library as an atomic block and assumes libraries are otherwise exchangeable, so systematic library-level confounding not removed by the design (e.g., batch correlated with dissection order) would distort the null in ways the permutation cannot capture. The pseudo-region negative control bounds this possibility empirically: in a structure-free re-run of the same test on 127,756 pseudo-pairs, the marginal tail rates (5.79% lower, 6.87% upper) closely match the real analysis (6.17%/7.90%), so the observed over-dispersion is attributable to library-level grouping shared by both designs rather than to null-width mismatch (Results; Additional file 1: Note 4.6, Fig. S14). In addition, B = 1,000 permutations bounds the resolvable P-value at 9.99 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074; because the BH threshold for the smallest of m = 31,764 ordered P-values is 0.05/31,764 \u2248 1.6 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076, detecting FDR-significant per-pair signals at B = 1,000 would require ~635 of the 31,764 P-values to sit simultaneously at the permutation floor\u2014a regime the empirical FDR analysis shows is not approached\u2014or B \u2248 6 \u00d7 10\u2075 permutations (or a hierarchically structured multiplicity model). Because per-signal tests share cell types and regions, BH correction over all pairs is itself approximate; BH-FDR is applied separately within each dataset, so significance thresholds are not directly comparable across datasets, and cross-dataset meta-analysis would require a unified multiple-testing framework.')

p('Future directions include developmental biology (quantifying functional differentiation between developmental stages), drug response profiling (measuring selectivity of drug-induced transcriptional changes), aging research (tracking age-related baseline vs. functional transcriptional drift), and evolutionary cell biology (quantifying conservation and divergence of cell-type programs across the tree of life). The CKI Python package (v0.4.6) and all analysis notebooks are available at https://github.com/zhanglknt/CKI-cell-type-identification under the MIT License.')

# ============================================================
# CONCLUSIONS (GB: required, before Methods)
# ============================================================
heading('Conclusions', level=1)
p('CKI adapts the baseline-normalization logic of Ka/Ks to single-cell transcriptomics, decomposing population divergence into a housekeeping baseline rate (k_n) and an identity-gene functional rate (k_f). Across simulation, calibration, cross-organ, pan-cancer, and brain-regional analyses, we showed that \u03c9 rankings carry reproducible biological signal while absolute \u03c9 values remain scheme- and dataset-specific, that statistical claims at atlas scale require nulls respecting the experimental design, and that the absence of FDR-significant candidates is itself an informative bound on what adult transcriptomes alone can support. CKI is released as an open-source Python package with a fully reproducible analysis pipeline, and we anticipate its use as an interpretable complement to standard distance metrics in comparative single-cell analyses.')

# ============================================================
# MATERIALS AND METHODS (NAR: placed BEFORE Results)
# ============================================================
heading('Methods', level=1)

heading('CKI computation', level=2)
p(f'We normalize raw count matrices to 10,000 counts per cell and apply log1p transformation [42]. Pseudobulk vectors average expression across cells sharing the same cell-type annotation (at least 10 cells per group). Housekeeping (HK) genes are loaded from the HRT Atlas v1.0 reference [13] ({_ds["hrt_atlas_n_hk"]:,} human-mouse conserved HK genes; mouse ortholog column for mouse datasets, human column for Tabula Sapiens, TCGA, and the brain atlas). The package also supports data-driven auto-detection (detect_housekeeping_genes(); combined detection-rate and CV criterion, use_reference = False), but all reported analyses use the pre-specified reference.')

p('For populations A and B with pseudobulk vectors \u03b5_A and \u03b5_B, each vector is normalized to a probability distribution before Jensen-Shannon (JS) divergence computation by a softmax over log-transformed values, which in count space is exactly the operation of adding a +1 pseudo-count followed by L1 normalization: p_i = (c_i + 1)/\u03a3_j(c_j + 1), where c denotes the pseudobulk count. The aggregation order differs across datasets and must be distinguished: the brain pipeline applies log1p after averaging counts, softmax(log1p(mean counts)), matching the count-space formula exactly, whereas the mouse (Tabula Muris pilot) and human (Tabula Sapiens) pipelines average per-cell log1p values and normalize afterwards, softmax(mean(log1p)). This difference plausibly contributes, alongside dataset scale and gene-selection effects, to the non-transferability of the dataset-specific calibration constants (Results). Then k_n = JS(norm(\u03b5_A[H]), norm(\u03b5_B[H])), where H is the set of HK gene indices; k_f = JS(norm(\u03b5_A[I]), norm(\u03b5_B[I])), where I is the set of top-2,000 highly variable genes (HVGs; Seurat flavor) excluding HK genes; \u03c9 = k_f/k_n, with JS divergence [43] using the base-2 logarithm (range [0, 1]). To guard against division by near-zero k_n, the package exposes an optional denominator floor (kn_floor); the package default (kn_floor = 0) applies only a positivity guard, used by all reported single-cell analyses, so no reported single-cell \u03c9 was capped (minimum observed per-pair k_n: 1.1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074, mouse; 6.3 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074, human; 9.2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2075, brain; only 1 of 31,764 pairs had k_n below 1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074, and these values entered \u03c9 uncapped). The TCGA bulk analysis is the sole exception and applies kn_floor = 1 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074, because pseudobulk averaging across millions of cells compresses HK gene variance and drives aggregate k_n toward zero (see Results).')

p('A note on package parity: all hybrid-scheme analyses reported in this paper (mouse pilot, Tabula Sapiens, TCGA, brain atlas) select the k_f genes of each pair as the top-200 genes ranked by the absolute pseudobulk difference. The released package\u2019s func_method = "pairwise_de" option instead runs a two-directional Wilcoxon test and yields a different gene set; the reported abs-diff scheme is provided exactly as func_method = "pairwise_absdiff" in package version 0.4.6. All \u03c9 values reported here were computed with the abs-diff scheme. The package\u2019s permutation test (bootstrap_test()) reproduces the reported null by default: reselect_identity = True holds the HK set fixed while re-selecting the k_f set at every permutation under the same per-pair rule as the observed value; the legacy fixed-gene-set null remains available as reselect_identity = False and is anti-conservative relative to re-selection (cf. the TCGA fixed-panel caveat).')

heading('Dimensionality invariance of JS divergence', level=2)
p(f'Because k_n is computed on ~1,130 HK genes and k_f on 200\u20132,000 HVG genes, we verified that JS divergence is not systematically biased by gene set dimensionality. A simulation of {int(_phaseC_dim.get("n_trials", 2000)):,} random Dirichlet distribution pairs across dimensions 50\u20135,000 showed mean JS divergence effectively constant (0.155\u20130.159, ratio = {_phaseC_dim.get("ratio_2000_to_1130", 1.001):.3f} between d = 1,130 and d = 2,000; Additional file 1: Fig. S12). The systematic inflation of k_f relative to k_n (\u03c9 = 6.67 for equivalent populations) therefore arises from HVG selection bias rather than dimensional mismatch; the calibrated omega (\u03c9_cal = \u03c9 / 6.67) absorbs this bias into the empirical baseline, and the permutation null\u2014constructed using the same gene sets\u2014ensures internal consistency.')

heading('Permutation test', level=2)
p('Statistical significance was assessed with permutation tests whose exchangeability unit and gene-selection handling follow the structure of each dataset. For the mouse pilot, Tabula Sapiens, and TCGA per-cancer analyses, labels were permuted between groups (cell labels, or tumor/normal labels across bulk samples; B = 1,000); pseudobulks were recomputed after each permutation and \u03c9 recalculated under the same per-pair gene-selection procedure as the observed value, so the null incorporates the gene-selection step. The TCGA per-cancer permutation test additionally holds the identity gene set fixed across permutations (a global HVG panel selected once on the observed grouping); because this fixed-panel null can only deflate the null k_f relative to re-selection, it is anti-conservative, and the corresponding TCGA P-values (all P > 0.9) should be read with this caveat. For the brain atlas, the authoritative null is the block-shuffle permutation described under \u201cMultiplicative residual model\u201d (10x libraries permuted across regions, per-pair genes re-selected at every permutation; B = 1,000). Empirical P-values use the one-sided upper-tail formula P = (count(\u03c9_null \u2265 \u03c9_obs) + 1)/(B + 1), where the +1 pseudocount avoids P = 0; the brain per-pair screen instead uses the one-sided lower-tail formula (anomalously low \u03c9), with the upper tail reported as a complementary summary. The one-sided default is appropriate because our hypothesis is directional: we test whether observed \u03c9 exceeds the null expectation, not whether it differs in either direction. Standardized effect size (SES = (\u03c9_obs \u2212 mean(\u03c9_null)) / sd(\u03c9_null)) is reported as a non-parametric descriptive statistic complementing the permutation P-value, not a parametric test statistic such as Cohen\u2019s d. Benjamini-Hochberg (BH) FDR correction [44] is applied within each dataset; larger-scale analyses are supplemented with non-parametric tests and descriptive statistics.')

heading('Datasets', level=2)
p(f'Tabula Muris FACS SmartSeq2 [8]: {_ds["tabula_muris_cells"]:,} cells, {_ds["tabula_muris_genes"]:,} genes, {_ds["tabula_muris_organs"]} organs (liver, kidney, spleen, lung, heart, bone marrow). Post-quality-control (QC): {_ds["tabula_muris_ct_entries"]} cell-type entries (each with at least 20 cells and at least one mouse contributing at least 10 cells), yielding C(38, 2) = 703 analyzed pairs. Highly variable genes selected using scanpy [45] with flavor="seurat" [46,47] and n_top_genes={_ds["n_hvg"]:,}.')

p(f'Tabula Sapiens v1.0 [9]: accessed via CZ CELLxGENE Discover. Post-QC: {_ds["tabula_sapiens_cells"]:,} cells ({_ds["tabula_sapiens_organs"]} h5ad files total), {_ds["tabula_sapiens_genes"]:,} genes, {_ds["tabula_sapiens_ct_entries"]} cell-type entries across 6 organs. Of these, {_h["n_ct_analyzed"]} entries passed the pairwise-analysis filters (at least 20 cells per entry and at least one donor with at least 10 cells; "unknown" annotations excluded), yielding C({_h["n_ct_analyzed"]}, 2) = {_h["n_pairs_total"]:,} analyzed pairs. HK genes: HRT Atlas v1.0 reference ({_ds["hrt_atlas_n_hk"]:,} genes; human column).')

p(f'TCGA bulk RNA-seq [48]: five cancer types from NCI Genomic Data Commons, accessed via TCGAbiolinks [49] and cBioPortal [50] APIs. LUAD: 495 tumor + 76 normal; LUSC: 567 tumor + 58 normal; LIHC: 365 tumor + 57 normal; KIRC: 755 tumor + 82 normal; BRCA: 1032 tumor + 109 normal. TPM values from UCSC Xena; within each cancer type, genes with mean expression below 0.5 TPM were removed, and the retained values were log2(TPM + 1) transformed. BRCA PAM50 subtype assignments [16,17] were retrieved from cBioPortal (brca_tcga_pub study, PAM50_SUBTYPE clinical attribute; 522 samples with subtype calls). LIHC Edmondson grade [15]: from cBioPortal, 288 tumors. LUAD mutations: from cBioPortal, 492 samples (61 EGFR, 120 KRAS, 311 WT).')

p('Human brain atlas [12]: Siletti et al. (2023) single-nucleus RNA-seq (v3.11) from CZ CELLxGENE Discover (collection ID: 283d65eb-dd53-496d-adb7-7570c7caa443). We used the Nonneurons.h5ad dataset (888,263 nuclei, 59,480 genes, 108 brain regions), classified by supercluster_term annotation into 10 major non-neuronal classes; after filtering (>= 20 nuclei per (region, cell_type) group, >= 50 per region), 886,808 nuclei contributed to the analysis (astrocytes 155,025; oligodendrocytes 490,246; oligodendrocyte precursors 105,723; committed oligodendrocyte precursors 4,118; microglia 91,826; vascular cells 9,586; fibroblasts 8,897; ependymal cells 5,779; choroid plexus 7,643; Bergmann glia 7,965). Pseudobulks were computed as cell-count-weighted means of per-library (10x sample) mean expression vectors per group, then normalized (Scanpy normalize_total, target_sum = 10,000) followed by log1p at the pseudobulk level. CKI \u03c9 was computed for all same-cell-type cross-region comparisons (31,764 pairs) with the hybrid scheme; HK genes came from the HRT Atlas v1.0 reference (1,115 genes matched to the Siletti annotation). For computational efficiency, per-pair identity-gene selection was restricted to a pre-filtered pool of the 5,000 non-HK genes with the highest mean expression, within which the top-200 genes with the largest absolute pseudobulk difference were selected per comparison (HK genes excluded).')

heading('Method comparison', level=2)
p(f'We computed five metrics on all {_h["n_pairs_total"]:,} Tabula Sapiens cell-type pairs: CKI \u03c9 (hybrid scheme), raw JS divergence (all genes), Spearman distance (1 - \u03c1), cosine distance (1 - cos \u03b8), and marker Jaccard distance (1 - Jaccard index of top-200 expressed genes). To avoid donor-pair proliferation, one pseudobulk was computed per cell-type entry from its largest donor (the donor with the most QC-passing cells); the method comparison therefore does not capture inter-donor variability. Inter-metric Spearman correlations and cell-type classification ROC-AUC were computed using scikit-learn.')

heading('Multiplicative residual model for brain regional analysis', level=2)
p('For the brain regional analysis, we designed a multiplicative model to detect (cell_type, region_pair) combinations with anomalously low \u03c9: expected_\u03c9 = \u03bc_ct \u00d7 \u03bc_pair / \u03bc_grand, where \u03bc_ct is the cell type\u2019s global mean \u03c9, \u03bc_pair the region pair\u2019s mean \u03c9, and \u03bc_grand the global mean over all 31,764 pairs (38.55); the multiplicative residual = observed / expected, and a residual well below 1 indicates the cell type is far less differentiated between those regions than expected from its global plasticity and the pair\u2019s overall divergence. Three confidence tiers were defined: Strong (residual < 0.3, \u03c9 < 15, lowest \u03c9 in the region pair), Moderate (residual < 0.5, \u03c9 < 25), and Weak (residual < 0.75, \u03c9 < 35). Statistical significance was assessed with a block-shuffle permutation null that preserves the joint cell-type \u00d7 region design: 10x Chromium libraries (sample_id) were treated as blocks and the sample-to-region assignment randomly permuted across libraries (preserving per-region library counts), after which region pseudobulks, all 31,764 pair \u03c9 values, and residuals were recomputed; because each library\u2019s cells move together, this null retains library-level structure while breaking the cell\u2013region association and is markedly more conservative than per-cell label shuffling. B = 1,000 permutations were run (minimum resolvable P = 1/1,001 \u2248 9.99 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074). Per-pair empirical P-values used the one-sided lower-tail formula P = (count(\u03c9_null \u2264 \u03c9_obs) + 1)/(B + 1), appropriate because candidates are defined by anomalously low \u03c9; the complementary upper-tail P-value was computed for every pair and both tails are reported. Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction was applied across all m = 31,764 pairs. No pair reached q < 0.05 (minimum q = 0.520); at this multiplicity q < 0.05 would require B \u2248 6 \u00d7 10\u2075 permutations or ~635 P-values at the permutation floor (see Statistical reporting), so this outcome reflects permutation resolution rather than evidence against the candidates. The 31 Strong-tier pairs with raw P < 0.05 are reported as hypothesis-generating signals, prioritized for future lineage-tracing validation, with interpretation restricted to the predefined Strong tier rather than the full 31,764 search space. A cell-type-level test additionally asked whether regional structure significantly raises a cell type\u2019s mean \u03c9 relative to the same null (one-sided upper-tail P across B = 1,000 permutations). As a negative control for null calibration, the same test was re-run on pseudo-regions obtained by splitting each region\u2019s libraries uniformly at random into two halves (seed fixed; 127,756 pseudo-pairs across the ten classes; Additional file 1: Note 4.6).')



heading('Robustness and calibration analyses', level=2)
p('Donor confounding and within-donor gradient (brain). The brain atlas contains nuclei from four donors, and 94.5% of region pairs share at least one donor (median top-donor share within a region: 0.61). To test whether the pooled class-level gradient reflects donor identity rather than regional biology, we recomputed cross-region \u03c9 using only same-donor (donor, region) pseudobulk pairs: every pair of regions with at least 20 nuclei from the same donor contributed one comparison, and class means were aggregated over all such pairs (astrocytes: 11,139 pairs from 261 donor-region blocks; Bergmann glia: 10 pairs). k_n estimator sensitivity (brain). For all 31,764 pairs we compared four aggregation schemes: per-pair k_n (the reported estimator), an aggregate-first estimator (k_n computed once per cell type from class-level region pseudobulks), a global-k_n variant (a single grand-mean k_n shared by all classes), and k_f-only and k_n-only orderings; agreement was summarized as Spearman rank correlation of the ten class means. Scheme-matched split-half calibration. To test whether the mouse-derived calibration factor transfers, we repeated the random-split-of-the-same-population calibration inside each large single-cell dataset using exactly the gene-selection and pseudobulk pipeline of the corresponding analysis. Brain: for each cell class, the three regions with at least 200 nuclei were split into random halves (B = 50 splits per population; 29 populations), and 95% bootstrap CIs were obtained by resampling the 29 population means (the between-population bootstrap carries the variance relevant to the baseline, so the smaller per-population split count does not inflate the reported CIs relative to the B = 1,000 used in the main inference). Tabula Sapiens: for each (organ, cell type) group from the largest donor with at least 100 cells passing QC (71 populations across six organs), cells were randomly split into halves (B = 50) and \u03c9 computed with the same per-pair hybrid pipeline as the main Tabula Sapiens analysis. Lineage enrichment and tier sensitivity (brain). Enrichment of oligodendrocyte-lineage classes among Strong candidates was tested with a hypergeometric test over the 31,764 pairs (12,775 belonging to the lineage), corroborated by permutation (B = 100,000); tier-threshold sensitivity was assessed by recomputing the Strong set and lineage enrichment over a grid of residual caps (0.2\u20130.4) and \u03c9 caps (12\u201325).')

p('Donor-stratified null (brain). To ask whether the class-level significance of the pooled block-shuffle null survives when library-to-region assignments cannot cross donor boundaries, we re-ran the permutation test with a donor-stratified shuffle: within each cell class, the sample_id-to-region assignment was permuted only among libraries belonging to the same donor (B = 1,000; identical gene set, pseudobulks, and \u03c9 pipeline as the free shuffle). This scheme preserves each donor\'s library counts and the per-region block-size structure within donors while breaking the library-to-region association; libraries from donors contributing a single library to the class (non-shufflable blocks; e.g., 8 of 606 astrocyte libraries) were held fixed, and permutations returning the identity assignment were retained as valid draws. Class-level significance used the same one-sided upper-tail statistic, P = (count(mean \u03c9_null \u2265 mean \u03c9_obs) + 1)/(B + 1). The accompanying free-null P-values come from an independent Monte-Carlo re-run within the same script (same B and pipeline), so small differences from the primary analysis (ependymal cells P = 0.058 here versus 0.075; committed oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) P = 0.004 versus 0.005) reflect Monte-Carlo variability; the donor-stratified null is strictly the more conservative test.')

heading('Ground-truth simulation', level=2)
p('To measure specificity and sensitivity against a known ground truth, we injected perturbations of known magnitude into a real single-cell background: Tabula Muris FACS marrow B cells (1,848 cells). Each replicate resampled two independent groups of 200 cells (gene set: 1,064 matched HK genes plus the 5,000 non-HK genes with the highest global means, mirroring the brain pipeline). A functional signal was injected as a multiplicative shift of 2^\u03b4 (\u03b4 = 0.125\u20132) on a fixed module of 200 non-HK genes in group B; neutral perturbations were injected separately as a 2^\u03b7 shift (\u03b7 = 0.25\u20131) on HK genes in group A (neutral drift that should not count as functional divergence) or Poisson noise across all genes in group A (\u03b5 = 0.3\u20131, technical batch noise). Six metrics were computed per replicate with the identical code path as the brain analysis (\u03c9, k_f, k_n, raw JS divergence over the full kept gene set, cosine distance, k_f/k_total). Signal scenarios were repeated with three independent random module draws (seeds 42, 137, 2024); detection thresholds were calibrated per metric as the 95th percentile of 200 baseline replicates, so type-I error and power refer to a common nominal level (the 95th-percentile threshold estimate itself carries Monte Carlo noise of roughly \u00b11.5 percentage points at 200 baseline replicates, small relative to the between-metric gaps reported). Robustness scenarios (30% dropout, twofold depth difference, fourfold cell-count imbalance) and module-size sensitivity (m = 50, 200, 500) were run at \u03b4 = 0.25 and \u03b4 = 1. The entire design was repeated in a second background within the same Tabula Muris FACS platform\u2014skin keratinocyte stem cells (1,371 cells)\u2014with identical grids, module seeds, group sizes, and code path (1,750 replicates per background across the full grid). Full results: Additional file 1: Note 3.12 and the results/groundtruth_simulation_*.csv files of the companion repository.')

heading('Perturbation demonstration (IFN-\u03b2 PBMC)', level=2)
p('To test \u03c9 on a real perturbation with known ground truth, we re-analyzed the droplet arm of Kang et al. [14] (GEO: GSE96583): peripheral blood mononuclear cells from eight donors, split into control and 6-hour IFN-\u03b2-stimulated conditions and captured in two 10x lanes, with donor assignment by genetic demultiplexing as provided by the original authors. All control cells sit in one lane and all stimulated cells in the other, so condition is fully confounded with capture lane (disclosed in Note 3.15): every metric inherits the same confound, and cross-metric contrasts, not absolute detection, are the informative quantity. Singlets with annotated cell types were retained (24,413 cells across six cell types; Ensembl gene identifiers mapped to HGNC symbols). Pseudobulks were computed per (donor, condition) from raw counts, normalized to 10,000 counts, and log1p-transformed; \u03c9, k_f, k_n, and raw JS were evaluated with the per-pair top-200 scheme for two comparison classes: stimulated-versus-control within donor (perturbation class) and donor-versus-donor within condition (donor-drift class). A split-half baseline (six random half-splits per group) anchored the calibrated scale. The significance of the perturbation effect was assessed by permuting condition labels within donor (B = 1,000; genes re-selected at every permutation, one-sided upper tail), and class separability was summarized as the exact rank AUC of each metric within each cell type. Script: notebooks/79_kang_ifnb_demo.py; outputs: results/kang_ifnb_demo_pairs.csv, results/kang_ifnb_demo_summary.json.')

heading('Fixed gene-panel ablation', level=2)
p('To test whether the brain conclusions depend on the per-pair circular selection of k_f genes (top-200 genes ranked by the absolute difference of the same two pseudobulks on which k_f is computed), we recomputed the entire observed brain landscape\u2014all 31,764 pairs, with identical keep gene set, pseudobulks, and k_n\u2014under three alternative gene-selection schemes: (i) a fixed panel of the 2,000 non-HK genes with the highest global mean expression (selected once, pair-independent); (ii) a leave-pair-out panel, in which the top-200 genes for a pair are selected by the mean absolute pseudobulk difference over all other region pairs of the same cell type (adaptive but not circular for the tested pair); and (iii) all 5,000 non-HK genes of the keep set. The reference implementation reproduced the reported landscape exactly (maximum per-pair |\u0394\u03c9| = 6.4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u00b3). We summarized pair-level and class-level rank agreement (Spearman), the circularity inflation as the per-pair ratio of k_f under the reported scheme to k_f under each alternative, and the agreement of the multiplicative-residual ranking. A scheme-matched block-shuffle null (B = 200; minimum resolvable P \u2248 0.005) was rerun under the leave-pair-out scheme to verify that class-level significance does not depend on circular selection. Full results: Additional file 1: Note 3.13 and the results/fixed_panel_ablation_* files of the companion repository.')

heading('Clinical severity analysis', level=2)
p('For TCGA clinical severity analysis, we computed intratumoral \u03c9 for samples within each clinical stratum using the hybrid scheme. BRCA PAM50 subtype calls [16,17] were retrieved directly from the cBioPortal API (brca_tcga_pub study, PAM50_SUBTYPE clinical attribute; 522 samples with subtype calls, cached locally for reproducibility, of which 506 had matched expression data and entered the analysis); no de novo centroid-based classification was performed in this work. LIHC Edmondson grades [15] came from cBioPortal (n = 288 tumors), and LUAD mutation status (EGFR, KRAS, WT) from cBioPortal (n = 492 samples). Between-stratum differences were tested with Kruskal-Wallis (PAM50 subtypes, LUAD mutations) and trend with Jonckheere-Terpstra (Edmondson grades). Paired versus unpaired tumor-normal comparisons are reported as descriptive statistics (medians and interquartile ranges, IQRs) without formal P-values, as the paired design does not meet the independence assumption of standard between-group tests. k_f-only and k_n component controls for the ordering claims were computed with the identical pipeline (per-cancer loading, gene filtering, and TT-pair subsampling), stratifying per-tumor mean k_f and k_n exactly as for \u03c9; per-cell-type mean k_f for the cross-organ ranking was computed from the same pseudobulks and per-pair gene selection (notebooks/83_kf_only_ordering.py; results/kf_only_ordering.csv; results/kf_only_severity.csv; Additional file 1: Note 3.16).')

heading('Computational environment', level=2)
p('Typical runtime for a single cell-type pair is under 5 minutes on a standard laptop; the full brain analysis (31,764 pairs) required approximately 72 core-hours on a Windows x64 workstation with at least 32 GB RAM (the verified environment of the Reproducibility Guide, Section 1.3). All analyses were performed in Python 3.14.4 with scanpy 1.12.1 [45], scipy >= 1.10.0, numpy >= 1.23.0, pandas >= 1.5.0, matplotlib >= 3.6.0, seaborn [51] >= 0.12.0, and scikit-learn [52] >= 1.2.0; all random seeds were fixed at 42. Permutation results are stable with respect to seed choice: with B = 1,000 permutations the Monte Carlo standard error of the empirical P-value is approximately 0.016 at P = 0.5, so seed variation has negligible impact on statistical conclusions.')

heading('Statistical reporting', level=2)
p('We report summary statistics as mean \u00b1 s.d. (range) or median [IQR] as noted. Resampling-based inference [53] (permutation tests for P-values; bootstrap for confidence intervals) was performed for all four datasets with B = 1,000: label permutation for the mouse pilot (15 cell-type pairs, 6 calibration controls), human Tabula Sapiens, and TCGA, and the block-shuffle null for the brain atlas (Methods). Empirical P-values are one-sided, P = (count(\u03c9_null \u2265 \u03c9_obs) + 1)/(B + 1), with the exchangeability unit following the dataset structure; in each iteration pseudobulks are recomputed from the permuted groups and \u03c9 recalculated with identity genes re-selected on the permuted pseudobulks (the TCGA per-cancer permutation test is the sole exception, holding a fixed HVG panel; Methods). Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction [44] is applied within each dataset, with two levels distinguished. First, group-level tests (one test per cell class, cell type, or cancer type): brain cell-class (m = 10), human per-cell-type (m = 17), mouse pilot (m = 15), TCGA per-cancer (m = 5); the BH thresholds for the most significant test (0.05/m) are 5.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b3, 2.9 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b3, 3.3 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b3, and 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b2\u2014all above the minimum resolvable permutation P-value of 9.99 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074 at B = 1,000, so resolution is sufficient for every group-level test. Second, the brain per-pair screen, with BH correction across all m = 31,764 region pairs: the BH threshold for the smallest ordered P-value (0.05/31,764 \u2248 1.6 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076) lies roughly 600-fold below the smallest resolvable P, so q < 0.05 is unattainable unless ~635 of the 31,764 P-values sit at the permutation floor (a regime the empirical FDR analysis shows is not approached) or B \u2248 6 \u00d7 10\u2075 permutations are run. The per-pair FDR outcome (minimum q = 0.520) is consequently a statement about permutation resolution rather than evidence against any candidate, and we report it together with the raw permutation P-value distribution (1,960 of 31,764 pairs at raw P < 0.05, slightly more than the ~1,588 expected under a global null).')

p('Bootstrap 95% confidence intervals for \u03c9 point estimates were computed by resampling observed pair-level \u03c9 values with replacement (B = 10,000) and reporting the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. Because pairs are nested within regions, we additionally computed region-clustered block bootstrap 95% CIs (B = 2,000; the 108 regions resampled with replacement, all landscape statistics recomputed per resample): gradient 6.10 [5.55, 9.63], grand mean \u03c9 38.55 [36.35, 40.73], Strong-candidate count 39 [12, 74]; these cluster-aware intervals are wider and are the preferred uncertainty summary for landscape-level quantities. Per-class calibrated quantities additionally propagate the uncertainty of the calibration denominator: a joint region-clustered bootstrap (B = 5,000) resamples regions with replacement for the numerator (region-clustered weighted resampling, pair weights equal to the product of the two regions\u2019 library counts) while independently resampling the split-half control populations for the denominator (two-stage: control populations first, random half-splits within each drawn population second), and reports the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the ratio; this joint interval supersedes the earlier i.i.d. interval, which propagated neither source and was anti-conservative (Additional file 1: Note 3.5; notebooks/81_perclass_uncertainty.py). The \u03c9 distribution was characterized using skewness, excess kurtosis, and normality tests (Shapiro-Wilk for n \u2264 5,000; D\u2019Agostino-Pearson for n > 5,000): all distributions were right-skewed (brain 2.22; mouse pilot 0.99; human 1.17), and normality was rejected for the large datasets (brain P < 2.2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b9\u2076; human P = 7.6 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2074\u00b2) but not for the mouse pilot (P = 0.071, minimal power at n = 15). The split-half calibration experiment (n = 6 random splits of the same tissue) yielded a mean \u03c9 = 6.67 (95% bootstrap CI [4.24, 9.24], B = 10,000), reflecting systematic inflation of k_f relative to k_n from identity-gene selection; the permutation test accounts for this by constructing the null under the same gene-selection procedure.')

p('Statistical conventions. All P-values are from one-sided permutation tests (B = 1,000 for mouse/human/TCGA/brain and for the multiplicative residual model block-shuffle null) unless otherwise specified. Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction was applied within each dataset. Non-parametric tests (Spearman correlation, Mann-Whitney U, Kruskal-Wallis, Jonckheere-Terpstra) are two-sided and reported with exact P-values, with one exception: the mouse-pilot X-versus-C calibration contrast (Fig. 2D) uses a one-sided Mann-Whitney U test (H1: \u03c9_X > \u03c9_C) matching the directional calibration hypothesis; as an exploratory pilot (n = 15) this contrast is reported for transparency rather than as a pre-registered test. Descriptive statistics are reported as mean \u00b1 SD or median [IQR] as indicated. Effect sizes include standardized effect size (SES = (\u03c9_obs \u2212 mean(\u03c9_null)) / sd(\u03c9_null)), computed from the permutation null distribution. Bootstrap 95% confidence intervals use B = 10,000 resamples. All analyses use JS divergence with base-2 logarithm. Sample sizes (n) are reported for each comparison.')

heading('Use of large language models', level=2)
